notes.body:
Zero trust is a set of design principles, not a product SKU. The core idea is simple and brutal: no user, device, application, or network segment is trusted by default — every access request is authenticated, authorized, and continuously re-evaluated. Location used to be trust. In the old castle-and-moat model, anything inside the corporate network was treated as friendly and anything outside as hostile. That model died the moment laptops left the building, VPNs became the front door, and attackers learned that phishing one employee puts them inside the moat. Zero trust moves the trust decision from 'where are you?' to 'who are you, what is the health of your device, and is this specific request legitimate right now?' NIST SP 800-207, the canonical reference, frames zero trust around a simple rule: all communication is secured regardless of network location, access is granted per session, and access decisions are driven by dynamic policy that considers client identity, the requesting asset's posture, and behavioral attributes. Notice what is missing from that list: 'because the request came from the office LAN.' There are three ideas people constantly confuse with zero trust, so let us separate them. First, zero trust is not 'no trust at all' — it is trust that is explicitly granted, narrowly scoped, and constantly re-verified. You still trust your identity provider, your policy engine, and your device health signals; you just stop trusting the network cable. Second, zero trust is not a firewall upgrade. A firewall is one enforcement point; zero trust is the architecture that coordinates all of them. Third, zero trust is not achieved by buying a platform — it is achieved by changing defaults: default-deny instead of default-allow, per-session authorization instead of login-once, and continuous diagnostics instead of annual audits. On the endpoint, the single most consequential default you can change is which applications are allowed to run. A default-allow endpoint runs whatever arrives; a default-deny endpoint runs only what policy explicitly permits. Everything ThreatLocker's allowlisting module does — and everything you will operate in this course — is zero trust applied to the question 'may this code execute on this machine?' Common operator gotcha: do not pitch zero trust to leadership or to an interview panel as a product. The panel on October 13 will respect you far more for saying 'zero trust is the discipline of removing implicit trust and enforcing per-session, policy-driven decisions, and here is how we applied it to application execution' than for naming vendors.

