<text x="636.0" y="104" font-family="ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,SF Mono,Menlo,Consolas,monospace" font-size="11" fill="#8b949e" text-anchor="middle" font-weight="400" opacity="1.0" class="a-node" style="animation-delay:3.85s">Note 4</text></svg><figcaption style="margin-top:.6em;color:#8b949e;font-size:.85em;text-align:center"><strong>Fig. IL29-M01-D1 — </strong>Read the notes in order — What &#x27;zero trust&#x27; actually means lays the foundation and Assume breach: the mindset that… brings it together. Each note assumes the vocabulary of the ones before it.</figcaption></figure>

NIST SP 800-207 gives zero trust a logical anatomy with three named components, and you should be able to draw them from memory. The Policy Engine (PE) is the brain: it makes the ultimate decision to grant, deny, or revoke access to a resource, using enterprise policy plus inputs like identity, device posture, and threat intelligence. The Policy Administrator (PA) is the muscle that carries out the decision: it establishes and tears down the communication path between the subject and the resource, for example by generating session credentials or configuring the enforcement point. The Policy Enforcement Point (PEP) is the gate: it sits in the data path, enables or terminates connections, and enforces the PE's decision. The PE and PA together form the control plane; the PEP and the resource sit in the data plane. A trust algorithm inside the PE weighs the inputs — identity assurance level, device compliance state, behavioral anomalies — and produces the decision. Worked example, mapped to an endpoint: an accountant double-clicks invoicing-app.exe. The endpoint agent acting as PEP intercepts the execution request instead of letting it run. It asks the control plane: the PE evaluates policy ('is invoicing-app.exe on the allowlist for the accounting group? is the device compliant? is the file hash unchanged?') and the PA instructs the PEP to allow or block the launch. If allowed, the PEP permits that specific execution; it does not hand the app a permanent hall pass. That is the per-session idea applied to process execution. Now map the failure modes, because operators live in failure modes. If the PEP is bypassed — the app launches without consulting policy — the whole model collapses regardless of how good the PE is. If the PE's inputs are stale — device posture from last week — decisions are made on lies. If the PA cannot reach the PEP — the agent is offline — you need a defined offline posture: fail closed (block new executions) or fail open, and you must choose deliberately, document it, and be able to defend the choice. Common gotcha: people draw the PE, PA, and PEP as three boxes and stop. The interview-grade understanding is the data flow between them and what happens when each link breaks. Practice explaining it in sixty seconds: subject requests, PEP intercepts, PE decides with the trust algorithm, PA enforces, PEP gates. Then add: 'and on the endpoint, the application-control agent is the PEP for the question of whether code may run.'

