Every security control is either default-allow (permit unless explicitly blocked) or default-deny (block unless explicitly permitted), and the choice decides who does the hard work: the defender or the attacker. Default-allow puts the burden on the defender to enumerate badness. Antivirus signatures, blocklists, firewall deny rules — all of these require you to know about the threat in advance, name it, and push the rule before the threat arrives. The attacker only needs one thing you have not seen yet: a fresh hash, a new domain, a renamed binary. This is an asymmetric game you lose by construction, because the space of 'unknown bad' is infinite and your blocklist is finite. Default-deny flips the burden. The policy enumerates goodness — the finite, knowable set of applications your business actually uses — and everything else is denied without needing to be recognized. The attacker's novel malware is not blocked because it is known-bad; it is blocked because it is not known-good, which requires zero threat intelligence about that specific sample. Worked example: a finance team uses 40 applications. Under default-allow, the defender must maintain rules against millions of malware families and hope the next one is recognized. Under default-deny, the defender maintains 40 allow entries and the next malware fails at the gate unrecognized — which is fine, because recognition was never required. Now the honest costs, because default-deny is not free. First, someone must build and maintain the allowlist: discover what is used, approve it, handle updates. Second, the first weeks are noisy: legitimate apps get blocked and users complain, so you need a fast approval workflow or the business routes around you. Third, allowlisting the application is necessary but not sufficient — a trusted app can be abused, which is exactly why ringfencing exists (Module M03) and why you should never present allowlisting as the whole answer. The classic mistake is treating default-deny as 'set and forget.' It is a living policy: every software update changes hashes, every new hire needs new apps, every acquired company brings unknown software. Operator habit: measure your policy's health by the approval queue's age. A queue of pending approvals older than your SLA is not a backlog; it is users learning to work around security. In the interview, when they ask why ThreatLocker leads with allowlisting, the answer is this economics argument in two sentences: default-allow forces defenders to enumerate infinite badness; default-deny enumerates finite goodness, so unknown threats fail closed with no signature required.

