Here is the truth that separates operators from tool buyers: in a deny-by-default world, the policy is the product. The agent is just the enforcement mechanism; the value — and the risk — lives in the rules you write, the approvals you grant, and the exceptions you allow to rot. A sloppy allowlist with a standing 'allow everything from the downloads folder' exception is default-allow wearing a default-deny costume. So the operational discipline has four parts. First, policy authorship: every rule needs an owner, a business justification, a scope (which users, which machines, which groups), and an expiry or review date. 'Allow chrome.exe' is not a rule; 'Allow Google Chrome stable, publisher Google LLC, for the Sales group, reviewed quarterly' is a rule. Second, change management: application updates change hashes, so your policy must handle trusted updaters — the vendor's own update mechanism — or every Patch Tuesday becomes a company-wide outage. You will design updater rules in M02; internalize now that update handling is the number-one operational burden of allowlisting and the number-one place junior operators get burned. Third, the approval workflow: when a user hits a block on legitimate software, the path from block to approval must be measured in minutes or hours, not days. A slow approval process teaches users that security is the department of 'no,' and they will find workarounds — personal devices, shadow IT — that are worse than the risk you were managing. Fourth, exception hygiene: every temporary exception ('allow this unsigned installer for the migration weekend') gets a ticket, an owner, and an automatic expiry. Review the exception list monthly; exceptions that outlive their justification become permanent holes. Worked example: the accounting office needs a new payroll app. The operator verifies the publisher certificate, checks the vendor's published hash, scopes the rule to the accounting group, sets a 90-day review, and documents the business justification in the ticket. The app updates itself monthly via its own updater — so the operator adds a trusted-updater rule for the vendor's signed updater rather than re-approving every version. When the panel asks 'how do you keep allowlisting from breaking the business,' this is the answer: updater rules for the churn, a fast approval lane for the new, scoped rules with reviews for everything, and exceptions that expire. Common gotcha: writing policy for the lab and forgetting policy for the humans. The workflow around the policy — who approves, how fast, what evidence — matters as much as the rules themselves.

