Application allowlisting lives or dies on its rollout, and the rollout has two modes you must never confuse. Monitor mode (sometimes called learning mode) observes: when an unknown binary executes, the agent logs the attempt — hash, path, parent process, user — but lets it run. Secured mode enforces: the same attempt is blocked. The safe path is always monitor first, secured second, and the reason is operational, not technical. On day one you do not know what the business actually runs. There are month-end close tools, quarterly tax utilities, the plant-floor label printer driver from 2014, and the manager's favorite PDF merger. Flip straight to Secured and you will block the payroll run on the 30th and spend the next week being hated. Monitor mode buys you the inventory: a complete, real-world list of what executes, built from observation instead of interviews (users always forget half their tools). Worked example: a 60-seat rollout. Weeks 1-4 run monitor mode, covering a full month-end close so the accounting batch jobs appear. Each Friday the operator reviews the unknown list: approves the legitimate (tax software update, new hire's scanner driver), investigates the suspicious (unsigned downloader in temp), and builds rules. By week 5 the unknown list is short and classified. Then the flip: Secured mode, but staged — pilot group first, then departments, with a rollback criterion ('if legitimate blocks exceed 5 per day in the pilot, we pause and review'). The emergency lane matters here: on flip day, a same-day approval path for anything wrongly blocked. Common gotcha: running monitor mode forever. Monitor mode is a phase, not a posture — an agent that only logs is a very expensive notepad. Set the flip date before you start monitoring, and treat 'we are still learning' after 60 days as a failure to decide. Second gotcha: monitoring too short a window. Two weeks misses the monthly jobs; monitor through at least one full business cycle. When the panel asks about your rollout experience, this staged story — monitor, classify, updater rules, staged enforcement — is what operator-grade sounds like.  The rollback criterion deserves the same rigor as the flip criteria. Define it before you start: for example, 'if legitimate blocks exceed five per user per day in the pilot group for two consecutive days, we return the pilot to monitor mode and review.' A rollback plan you write during the crisis is not a plan. Also decide who can authorize the flip in each direction — the same person who owns the policy — so enforcement decisions never stall waiting for a committee. Staged rollouts with written forward and reverse criteria are what separate a controlled deployment from an outage with good intentions.

