An allowlist is a list of identities, so the entire control reduces to one question: how do you identify a file reliably? There are three answers, each with a failure mode you must know. Hash identity (SHA-256 of the file bytes) is precise: it identifies exactly this version of this file, and any one-byte change — including a trojaned update — breaks the match and fails closed. The cost is churn: every vendor update changes the hash, so without updater rules you re-approve the world monthly. Certificate identity trusts the publisher: any binary signed by the vendor's code-signing certificate is allowed, which survives updates beautifully. The failure modes are real: a stolen vendor certificate lets malware ride the trust (this has happened to real vendors), and a legitimate-but-risky publisher gets a free pass for everything they sign. Path identity ('allow everything in C:\Program Files\Vendor\') is the weakest: it is cheap to write and trivially abused — malware simply lands in the allowed folder. Worked example: approving Google Chrome. The hash approach means re-approving every six-week release. The certificate approach — publisher Google LLC — auto-allows updates but also allows any other Google-signed binary. The path approach — allow C:\Program Files\Google\Chrome\* — lets an attacker drop evil.exe into that folder. The operator-grade answer is layered: certificate rule for the publisher plus a trusted-updater rule for the vendor's signed updater binary, with the updater constrained to replacing files the vendor owns. For unsigned legacy tools where no certificate exists, hash-pinning the specific approved version is the honest choice — precise, with documented churn cost. Common gotcha: certificate rules that are too broad. 'Allow anything signed by Microsoft' is effectively default-allow for the most-abused publisher on earth — scope certificate rules to the product or use them only for vendors with a clean, narrow signing practice. Second gotcha: forgetting that signatures can be valid but the file still malicious — a signed binary from a compromised vendor is allowed by your rule, which is why ringfencing (M03) constrains what allowed apps may do. Identity gets the app through the gate; behavior rules watch it inside.  In practice you verify signatures with the platform's own tooling: on Windows, Get-AuthenticodeSignature shows the signer and the chain status, and a Valid result means the signature is intact and chains to a trusted root — it does not mean the file is safe. Build the habit of checking the chain, not just the presence of a signature: a Valid signature from an unknown publisher is a fact to investigate, while a HashMismatch or NotSigned result on a file that claims to be vendor software is a finding. Record the signer subject and the thumbprint in your rule documentation so future-you can tell whether the vendor rotated their certificate or something changed unexpectedly.

