No operator hand-writes rules for every copy of Chrome on earth, which is why allowlisting platforms ship a built-in applications library: a curated catalog of known-good applications with pre-built rules. Think of it as the shared starting inventory — common business software already identified by hash sets or publisher certificates, ready to be approved into your policy with a click instead of a forensic exercise. The value is speed: on day one you can approve the standard stack (office suites, browsers, PDF readers, common utilities) from the library instead of discovering each binary the hard way. But the library is a starting point, not a verdict, and treating it as one is the classic junior mistake. Three cautions. First, the library knows common software, not your software: the accounting package, the plant-floor tools, the custom line-of-business app — those are yours to identify and rule. Second, library entries are someone else's judgment about what is safe; your policy still needs your scoping (which groups, which machines) and your review cadence. A library rule that allows a remote-access tool globally because 'it is a known application' is a policy failure, not a library failure. Third, libraries age: vendors change publishers, products get acquired, signing certificates rotate. Your change process must catch the drift. Worked example: rolling out to the accounting office from the lab. The library covers the office suite, the browser, the PDF reader, and the OS utilities — approve those in bulk with group scoping. The accounting package and the payroll tool are not in the library: for those you collect the installer, verify the publisher certificate against the vendor's published documentation, hash the approved version, and write your own rules with a review date. The unknown list from monitor mode then tells you what you missed: the scanner driver, the label printer software, the manager's PDF tool. Each gets the same treatment: verify, rule, scope, review date. Common gotcha: approving library entries without scoping. 'Allow TeamViewer (known app)' for the whole company because the library says it is legitimate is how you authorize shadow remote access. The library tells you what the software is; you decide who may run it.  Treat the library as a supply chain: ask who curates it, how often it updates, and what happens when a listed application is found to be compromised. Your change process should include a periodic review of library-sourced rules — quarterly is the working default — where you confirm the entries you depend on are still current and still scoped correctly. If the platform ever flags a library entry as revoked or untrusted, you need a runbook entry for that day: which of your rules referenced it, what breaks if you pull it, and what the replacement rule looks like. Depending on a shared catalog without a plan for its failure modes is outsourcing judgment without retaining oversight.

