Allowing an application to run says nothing about what it may launch, and attackers live in that gap. Parent-child process rules close it: every process creation is evaluated independently, so the parent's approval never transfers to the child. This is where allowlisting meets ringfencing, and you need both halves of the story. The allowlisting half: when winword.exe spawns powershell.exe, the agent checks powershell.exe against policy on its own merits. If PowerShell is not approved for the accounting group, the child is blocked even though the parent is allowed. The ringfencing half (full depth in M03): even when the child is an allowed application, the parent's ringfence policy can forbid the spawn — Word is simply not permitted to create child processes, period. Worked example, the invoice macro attack from M01: the macro instructs Word to launch a payload. Layer one: the payload binary is unknown, so allowlisting blocks the child at spawn — verdict: denied, not on the allowlist. Suppose instead the macro launches powershell.exe, which is a legitimate Windows binary. Layer two: PowerShell is allowlisted for the IT group but not for accounting, so the child is blocked by group scope. Suppose the attacker targets an IT workstation where PowerShell is allowed: layer three, the ringfence — Word's policy denies child process creation regardless of the child's identity, so the spawn is blocked and logged. Three independent evaluations, any one of which stops the chain. The operator's job is designing these rules deliberately: which applications may spawn children at all, and which children may they spawn? Browsers spawn renderers and updaters — expected. Word spawning cmd.exe — never expected. Common gotcha: the 'trusted parent' shortcut. 'Allow everything launched by our management agent' feels efficient and creates a single bypass for the entire policy — compromise the agent, inherit its trust. Children are evaluated on their own identity, always. Second gotcha: forgetting script interpreters. Blocking powershell.exe while allowing wscript.exe, mshta.exe, and rundll32.exe leaves the same capability under different names — child-process policy must cover the interpreter family, which is exactly the ringfencing design work you will do in M03.  Extend the same thinking beyond direct child processes. Scheduled tasks, services, WMI event subscriptions, and startup-folder entries are all ways to get code running without an obvious parent-child relationship — the persistence layer. Your policy should treat these as execution events subject to the same identity checks: a scheduled task launching an unknown binary is the same verdict as a user double-clicking it. When you audit an endpoint, enumerate these autostart locations alongside the running processes; attackers who cannot win the parent-child game will play the persistence game instead, and the allowlist must cover both boards.

