Ringfencing starts from an uncomfortable truth: allowlisting answers 'may this code run?' but says nothing about what the code may do once running. A trusted application is not a safe application — it is an application whose behavior you have not constrained yet. Ringfencing constrains it. For each allowed application, policy defines four behavior boundaries: which other applications or processes it may execute (child-process control), whether it may edit the registry and which keys, which files it may read and write and where, and which network sessions it may open. Anything outside the fence is denied and logged. The mental model is a playpen: the app is welcome inside, but the walls are real. Consider why this matters operationally. The most common endpoint attacks do not begin with an unknown binary — they begin with a trusted one. A Word document with a malicious macro, a browser exploit, a signed administrative tool turned against its owner: in every case the parent application is legitimate, allowed, and doing something it was never supposed to do. Allowlisting waves the parent through; ringfencing stops the abuse. Worked example: compare two policies for winword.exe. Policy A (allowlist only): Word may run. A macro instructs Word to launch powershell.exe with an encoded command — Word is allowed, PowerShell is a signed Windows binary, nothing stops the chain. Policy B (allowlist plus ringfence): Word may run, but its fence denies child-process creation, denies writes outside the user's document folders, denies registry edits, and denies network sessions. The same macro now fails four independent ways: the spawn is blocked, the payload write is blocked, the persistence registry key is blocked, and the callback is blocked. Each block is logged with full context — parent, attempted action, target — which becomes the alert feed you will triage in M07. Common gotcha: thinking of ringfencing as 'extra allowlisting.' It is a different question entirely. Allowlisting is identity (is this code approved?); ringfencing is behavior (is this action permitted for this app?). You need both because attackers defeat identity controls by borrowing trusted identities. Second gotcha: fencing everything identically. A browser legitimately needs network access; a PDF reader does not. The fence follows the application's legitimate function — least privilege applied per application, not per machine.  A subtle point: fences should inherit down the process tree. When a fenced application legitimately spawns an approved child — the browser's renderer, the updater — that child should run under a fence at least as strict as the parent's, not escape into an unfenced context. Otherwise the attacker only needs to compromise the allowed child to inherit its freedom. Define the expected child family per application explicitly, fence each member for its own function, and deny everything outside the family. The process tree is the unit of policy, not just the single process.

