The canonical ringfence-bypass pattern — the one you must be able to whiteboard — is Word calling PowerShell. Walk it slowly. The attacker crafts a document containing a malicious macro. The user opens it in Word, which is allowlisted: the gate asks 'may Word run?' and the answer is yes. The macro executes inside Word's process and instructs the operating system to launch powershell.exe with a command that downloads and runs a payload. Under allowlisting alone, every binary in this chain is legitimate: Word is approved, PowerShell is a signed Microsoft binary. The attack succeeds without a single 'unknown' file. This is living-off-the-land: the attacker brings no tools, only instructions for your tools. The family has many members — script interpreters (powershell.exe, wscript.exe, cscript.exe), system utilities (rundll32.exe, mshta.exe, regsvr32.exe), and admin tools — all signed, all commonly allowed, all capable of executing attacker logic when invoked with the right arguments. Ringfencing breaks the pattern at the behavior layer. Word's fence states: this application may not create child processes. The macro's spawn request arrives, the fence evaluates the behavior (not the identity of PowerShell), and denies it. Note the elegance: the decision does not depend on recognizing powershell.exe as dangerous — it depends on Word having no legitimate need to spawn children. Worked example with numbers you can defend: in a 200-seat office, Word legitimately spawns child processes approximately never — its function is document editing. So a deny-child-processes fence for Word has a near-zero false-positive rate and blocks the entire macro-to-shell attack class. Contrast with a developer workstation where the IDE legitimately spawns compilers: there the fence must allow the compiler family and deny everything else, which is more work but the same principle. Common gotcha: blocking powershell.exe by identity while leaving wscript.exe, mshta.exe, and rundll32.exe allowed. The attacker simply switches interpreters — you blocked a name, not the capability. The ringfence must cover the interpreter family as behaviors: 'this app may not spawn script interpreters,' not 'this app may not spawn powershell.exe.' Second gotcha: forgetting that the fence must also cover the payload write and the persistence. Defense in depth means the macro fails at the spawn, and if it somehow writes a file, the file-write fence stops the drop, and if that fails, the registry fence stops the Run key. Layer the behaviors; never rely on one.  The deeper variant of this pattern does not even need a new binary: the attacker abuses the legitimate binary's own arguments. The same powershell.exe that your admins use becomes the payload delivery vehicle when invoked with an encoded command — identity checks see the trusted binary, and only behavior analysis sees the attack. This is why mature ringfence design constrains not just which children may spawn but what arguments the sensitive ones may carry: script interpreters launched with encoded or hidden-window arguments by a document reader are never legitimate. When you write the fence, think in terms of behaviors — 'this app may not invoke script interpreters at all' — rather than binary names, because the names are the attacker's to choose.

