<text x="360.0" y="178" font-family="ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,SF Mono,Menlo,Consolas,monospace" font-size="11" fill="#8b949e" text-anchor="middle" font-weight="400" opacity="1.0" class="a-node" style="animation-delay:2.45s">Word-calling-PowerShell</text></svg><figcaption style="margin-top:.6em;color:#8b949e;font-size:.85em;text-align:center"><strong>Fig. IL29-M03-D2 — </strong>The vocabulary this module installs — ringfencing, behavior, control…. If you can define each term in one plain sentence, the module&#x27;s objectives are within reach.</figcaption></figure>

Some attacks do not even need a macro: they abuse the document handler chain itself. Consider the general class, taught here without vendor-specific exploit details: a specially crafted document, when merely previewed or opened, triggers an external protocol handler or helper application that the operating system launches automatically — and that helper, in turn, executes attacker-controlled commands. The user did not enable macros. The user did not click 'enable content.' The document format's own features did the work: the application that opened the document reached outside itself to invoke another program, and that program ran with the user's privileges. This class is why ringfencing covers more than child processes. A complete fence for a document reader constrains four behaviors against this pattern. First, child-process creation: the reader may not launch external handlers or helpers beyond its explicitly approved set. Second, the network session: even if a helper launches, it may not call out. Third, file writes: the helper may not drop payloads to disk. Fourth, registry edits: the helper may not establish persistence. Any one of these breaking the chain is sufficient; all four together make the class impractical. Worked example: design the fence for a PDF reader against handler-abuse. Child processes: deny all except the vendor's signed updater. Network: deny all — a reader's legitimate function (rendering documents) needs no network. File writes: allow only to the user's document folders; deny temp, startup, and system paths. Registry: deny all writes. Now walk the attack: the crafted PDF triggers a helper launch — blocked at child creation. Suppose the helper is somehow already present and invoked — its network call is blocked, its payload write is blocked, its persistence write is blocked. The document still renders for the legitimate user; the abuse class is fenced out. The operator lesson is about trust boundaries between applications: every time app A can invoke app B, that is a privilege boundary, and default-allow across it is the vulnerability. Ringfencing makes each such boundary default-deny. Common gotcha: assuming 'no macros' means 'no code execution.' Document formats are programs with rendering engines; the handler chain is the attack surface. Fence the behaviors, not just the macro setting.  Note that the preview pane counts as opening. Many document-abuse attacks trigger from the preview handler without the user ever double-clicking the file — the mere act of selecting the message renders the content through the vulnerable handler chain. Your user training should say 'do not preview unexpected attachments' rather than 'do not open them,' and your fence design should assume the handler runs on preview. This is also why the email gateway's attachment handling matters as a complementary layer: the fewer crafted documents that reach the inbox, the fewer handler invocations your fences must survive.

