PDF readers look harmless and are historically one of the most exploited application classes: complex parsers, JavaScript engines, and handler chains, all processing untrusted input. The fence for a PDF reader is the strictest of the three classes because the legitimate function is the narrowest: render documents. Child processes: deny entirely except the vendor's signed updater. A reader never legitimately launches other programs. Registry: deny all writes — a reader has no settings that require registry modification beyond its own keys, and persistence via the reader is never legitimate. File writes: allow only to user document folders for 'save as' functionality; deny temp, startup, and system paths absolutely. Network: deny entirely — rendering a document requires no network, and the updater gets its own narrow exception. This is the purest expression of least privilege per application: four denies and the app still does its job perfectly. Worked example: a crafted PDF attempts the handler-abuse pattern. It tries to invoke an external helper — child-process deny. It tries to execute embedded JavaScript that downloads a second stage — network deny. It tries to write the second stage to disk — file-write deny. It tries to register for persistence — registry deny. The user reads the invoice; the attack reads the fence. Now the validation discipline, because a fence you have not tested is a hope. Every ringfence policy gets a test plan before enforcement: for each deny, attempt the denied action with a harmless proof-of-concept on a test machine and confirm the block and the log. Attempt to spawn a child from the fenced app — confirm blocked and logged. Attempt the registry write — confirm blocked and logged. Attempt the file write to a denied path — confirm blocked and logged. Attempt the network session — confirm blocked and logged. Then attempt the legitimate workflows — open, edit, save, print, update — and confirm they work. Only then does the policy leave the pilot group. Common gotcha: testing only the blocks and not the legitimate workflows. A fence that blocks attacks and breaks work gets removed on Monday morning. Second gotcha: treating the PDF reader as 'not important' because it is not Word. Attackers do not share your ranking — they attack the reader precisely because defenders under-fence it. Fence it like it matters, because it does.  Two legacy Office features deserve explicit mention in your fence design: DDE and OLE. Both are mechanisms by which a document pulls in external content or launches linked objects — legitimate in 1998, abused ever since. If the business does not use them, disable them outright via policy rather than fencing around them; a disabled feature needs no fence. If some legacy workflow genuinely depends on them, that workflow gets the full treatment from M02: documented, scoped to the machines that need it, ringfenced tightly, with a migration plan and a date. 'Legacy requires it' is a bridge, not a destination.

