Just-in-time elevation is the replacement for standing admin: instead of the user holding administrative rights all day, the specific application or task is elevated for a scoped, time-bound need — then the privilege is gone. The user account itself never becomes an administrator. This distinction matters enormously. When you make someone a local admin, you grant a standing capability: every process they launch, every script they run, every piece of malware that lands in their session inherits administrative rights until someone remembers to remove them. When you elevate just the installer via policy, only that installer, launched for that task, holds the rights — the user's browser, email client, and everything else stay standard. The policy anatomy has five fields. Identity: which application may be elevated (publisher, hash, or path — the M02 identity discipline applies here too). Scope: which users or groups, on which machines. Conditions: when — business hours, ticket reference required, approval needed above a risk threshold. Duration: how long the elevation lasts — minutes for an installer, never permanent. Logging: every grant records who, what, why, when, and approved-by. Worked example: the monthly vendor driver update. Policy: 'VendorDriverSetup.exe, publisher Vendor Inc., may be elevated for the Helpdesk group on workstation OU, during business hours, with a ticket number, for 30 minutes, approved by the IT lead.' The technician runs the installer through the elevation workflow, it completes with admin rights, the window closes. The technician's account was standard before, during (outside that process), and after. Contrast with the old world: the technician is a local admin all month for a 10-minute task. Common gotcha: elevating the user 'temporarily' instead of the application. 'Temporary admin for the afternoon' is standing admin with a shorter memory — everything in the session inherits it, including the malware. Elevate the task, not the person. Second gotcha: elevation without conditions. An elevation rule with no ticket requirement, no time bound, and no approval is just admin rights with extra steps. The conditions are the control; write them tightly and review them quarterly.  Keep interactive elevation and non-interactive privilege strictly separated in your design. Services and scheduled tasks that run with stored credentials or as SYSTEM are not 'elevation' in the just-in-time sense — they are standing privilege by another name, and they get their own inventory, their own review, and their own hardening (managed service accounts, credential rotation, minimal rights). The just-in-time workflow covers the human asking for temporary rights; the service inventory covers the machine acting with permanent ones. Auditing one while ignoring the other leaves half the privilege surface unexamined.

