<text x="360.0" y="130" font-family="ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,SF Mono,Menlo,Consolas,monospace" font-size="11" fill="#8b949e" text-anchor="middle" font-weight="400" opacity="1.0" class="a-node" style="animation-delay:1.75s">elevation</text>
<text x="360.0" y="154" font-family="ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,SF Mono,Menlo,Consolas,monospace" font-size="11" fill="#8b949e" text-anchor="middle" font-weight="400" opacity="1.0" class="a-node" style="animation-delay:2.10s">elevate</text>
<text x="360.0" y="178" font-family="ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,SF Mono,Menlo,Consolas,monospace" font-size="11" fill="#8b949e" text-anchor="middle" font-weight="400" opacity="1.0" class="a-node" style="animation-delay:2.45s">task</text></svg><figcaption style="margin-top:.6em;color:#8b949e;font-size:.85em;text-align:center"><strong>Fig. IL29-M04-D2 — </strong>The vocabulary this module installs — local, admin, Just-in-time…. If you can define each term in one plain sentence, the module&#x27;s objectives are within reach.</figcaption></figure>

Elevation policies are written per application, because different software has different legitimate needs and the policy must match the need — no more, no less. Start by categorizing the elevation requests you will actually see. Installers and updaters: the most common legitimate need. Policy: the vendor's signed installer may elevate, scoped to the machines that need the software, time-bound to the deployment window. Management agents: backup, patching, and monitoring tools that genuinely require system-level access. Policy: the agent's service binary is elevated by design (services run as SYSTEM), but its interactive components are not — scope carefully. Administrative utilities: disk tools, driver installers, network diagnostics. Policy: named tools for named groups (IT staff), with ticket requirements for the sensitive ones. Legacy line-of-business apps: the painful category — old software that demands admin for no good reason. Policy: explicit elevation rules as a bridge, paired with a remediation plan (vendor engagement, compatibility shims, or replacement) and a review date, because 'legacy needs admin' cannot be a permanent answer. Worked example: build the policy set for a 25-seat office. The PDF reader's updater: signed updater, may elevate, all workstations, automatic — no ticket needed, it is routine. The accounting package installer: signed installer, may elevate for the IT group only, during the quarterly update window, ticket required. The disk-imaging tool: IT group only, ticket required, 2-hour window, approved by the IT lead. The legacy label-printer driver (unsigned, demands admin): hash-pinned, warehouse machines only, ticket required, 90-day review, vendor contacted about a signed version. Four applications, four different policies, each matching the actual need. Common gotcha: one elevation policy for everything. 'IT staff may elevate anything' recreates standing admin for the highest-value targets — attackers specifically hunt IT credentials. Even IT gets per-application policies, with the sensitive ones requiring approval. Second gotcha: forgetting service accounts and scheduled tasks. Elevation policy covers interactive elevation; services and scheduled tasks with stored credentials are a separate inventory with their own review — a service running as SYSTEM with a weak password is admin rights by another name.  Verify before you elevate. An elevation policy that approves 'the vendor installer' should still confirm the installer's integrity at approval time: compare its hash against the vendor's published value, confirm the signature chains correctly, and confirm you fetched it from the vendor's official distribution point. Elevating a trojaned installer with administrative rights is the worst possible outcome of this control — the policy that grants the privilege must also demand the evidence. Build the verification step into the intake form so it cannot be skipped under time pressure: no hash check, no elevation.

