The helpdesk elevation workflow is where policy meets humans, and its design decides whether the control survives. It has five steps, each non-optional. Intake: the requester submits who they are, what application needs elevation, the business reason, the ticket number, and the time window. 'Need admin plz' is not an intake — the form enforces the evidence. Identity verification: the helpdesk verifies the requester is who they claim — callback to the known number, in-person for sensitive requests, never trust the request channel alone (the 'urgent admin request' email from the 'CFO' is a classic social-engineering lure). Scoped approval: the approver checks the application against policy — is this app approved for elevation for this user? If yes and routine, approve; if sensitive or unprecedented, escalate to the security lead. The approval names the application, the machine, and the window — never 'admin for the afternoon.' Execution with logging: the elevation is granted through the agent, and the grant is logged with who, what, why, window, and approver. Post-hoc review: a sample of grants is reviewed weekly — was the business reason real, did the window match the need, any anomalies? Worked example: Tuesday 10 AM, a designer needs to install a font management tool. Intake: designer, FontTool Setup 4.2, vendor-signed, ticket HD-4821, today 10-12. Verification: helpdesk calls the designer's known extension. Approval: the tool is on the approved-elevation list for the design group — approved, 2-hour window, this machine. Execution: the installer elevates, completes, window closes. Review: Friday's sample includes HD-4821 — business reason confirmed, window matched. Total friction: one form and one callback. The emergency lane handles the 2 AM server case: on-call approver, same evidence, mandatory next-business-day review — emergencies get speed, not exemption. Common gotcha: verification theater. 'We verified identity' by replying to the same email thread that made the request proves nothing. Verify through an independent channel, every time, especially for sensitive elevations. Second gotcha: approval without expiry. An approved elevation that stays open 'until the work is done' is standing admin with paperwork. The window closes automatically; a new window needs a new approval.  Design the emergency lane with the same care as the standard path, because emergencies are when discipline is weakest. The lane needs three things: a reachable approver (the on-call rotation, not 'whoever is awake'), the same evidence minimum (ticket number, application identity, window — no evidence-free elevations, ever), and a mandatory next-business-day review where the grant is re-examined with cool heads. Publish the lane's usage statistics monthly: a lane used twice a quarter is healthy; a lane used twice a week is a standard path avoiding scrutiny, and the review will show it.

