Storage Control applies deny-by-default to the question every exfiltration attempt asks: can data move through this path? The module governs three things: storage devices (what may connect to the endpoint), file paths (where data may be written), and shares (who may read or write network storage). The device side is the most visible: USB mass storage sticks, external hard drives, phones and tablets connecting over MTP/PTP, optical media, and Bluetooth file transfer each get a policy — typically deny or read-only by default, with narrowly scoped exceptions. The path side constrains where even allowed writes may land: sensitive directories can be sealed against writes from removable sources, and application data folders can be fenced off from user-level copying. The share side extends the same logic to the network: file shares default to read-only, with read-write granted per group, per share, with justification. The unifying principle is that data movement is a privileged action, not a background capability. In the default-allow world, any process running as the user can copy any file the user can read to any device the OS recognizes — which is why the 'customer database on a USB stick' scenario is a one-line copy command. In the default-deny world, the copy hits the device policy before the first byte moves. Worked example: the Friday-afternoon insider. An employee decides to take the client list. They plug in a personal USB stick. The device class (USB mass storage) is deny-write by default for their group. The copy command fails; the attempt is logged with device ID, user, timestamp, and the file paths touched. The employee tries their phone instead — MTP storage class, same deny. They try uploading to personal cloud storage — the network egress policy (M06) denies the unapproved destination. Three paths, three denials, one alert bundle. The data never moved. Common gotcha: scoping device policy by machine instead of by user-and-machine. A 'blocked' USB stick that works when moved to another workstation is not a control; it is a suggestion. Policy must follow the device class and the user so the denial is consistent everywhere. Second gotcha: forgetting the read direction. Blocking writes stops exfiltration, but allowing reads from untrusted media invites malware in — the full policy addresses both directions, and the incident walkthrough later in this module assumes you have the logs for both.  Cover the read direction with equal seriousness. Blocking writes stops exfiltration, but unrestricted reads from untrusted media invite malware in — the stick found in the parking lot is the classic delivery vector. The complete device policy states both directions per class: the field tech's registered drive may read and write, the conference-room PC may read presentation drives but never write to them, and unknown media is denied both directions. When you write the policy table, give every class two columns — read and write — and force an explicit decision in each. Blank cells become holes.

