<text x="636.0" y="104" font-family="ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,SF Mono,Menlo,Consolas,monospace" font-size="11" fill="#8b949e" text-anchor="middle" font-weight="400" opacity="1.0" class="a-node" style="animation-delay:3.85s">Note 4</text></svg><figcaption style="margin-top:.6em;color:#8b949e;font-size:.85em;text-align:center"><strong>Fig. IL29-M05-D1 — </strong>Read the notes in order — What Storage Control governs:… lays the foundation and Legitimate carve-outs:… brings it together. Each note assumes the vocabulary of the ones before it.</figcaption></figure>

Device policy lives or dies on its class coverage, because attackers and careless users alike route around the classes you forgot. USB mass storage is the obvious one — the classic stick — and the default posture is deny-write, with read-only or full deny as the stricter variants. But the stick is only the beginning. Phones and tablets connecting over USB present themselves as MTP or PTP storage devices: to the OS they are storage, and 'I was just charging it' is not a policy exception — if it mounts as storage, the storage policy applies. External hard drives and SSDs are USB mass storage with more capacity and the same policy. Optical media (DVD-RW) still exists in some environments and gets its own rule. Bluetooth file transfer and even some wireless presentation dongles move files without a cable — the policy must name them or they become the hole. For each class, the policy states the default (deny, read-only, or allow), the exception roles, the exception conditions, and the review date. Worked example: the 40-seat company device table. USB mass storage: default deny-write; exception: field technicians with company-registered encrypted drives. Phones/tablets (MTP/PTP): default deny — charging-only cables issued to remove the ambiguity. External HDDs: default deny; exception: IT backup role with registered drives. Optical: default deny; no exceptions (no business need found in the audit). Bluetooth file transfer: default deny; exception: none — presentation uses the approved wireless system. Five classes, five rows, zero unaddressed paths. The audit that produces this table is non-optional: you cannot write policy for device classes you have not inventoried, so the first step is always discovering what connects to your endpoints — asset management data, helpdesk tickets about 'my drive doesn't work,' and a monitor-mode observation period. Common gotcha: the 'charging' loophole. Users will insist the phone was just charging. Issue charging-only cables (no data pins) and the argument evaporates — engineering beats arguing. Second gotcha: personal vs company devices. The policy distinguishes them explicitly: company-registered encrypted drives for the exception roles, personal devices never — because you cannot verify encryption, ownership, or malware status on a personal stick, and 'just this once' is how the database leaves.  You cannot govern devices you have not discovered, so the policy project starts with an inventory. Pull from asset management, but verify: check the helpdesk ticket history for 'my drive doesn't work' and 'can't access my phone' (each ticket names a device class in use), run a monitor-mode observation period logging every storage-device connection with its class and user, and survey the departments with legitimate needs (field ops, sales, executives). The inventory will surprise you — the lab equipment with its ancient data logger, the marketing team's camera cards — and each surprise is a row in the policy table that would otherwise have been a hole.

