Shares are storage too, and they get the same least-privilege treatment. The default posture for a network share is read-only for the groups that need it, with read-write granted narrowly, justified, and reviewed. Broad read-write shares are the insider's other favorite path: no USB stick required when the 'Finance' share lets every employee write — and the ransomware that encrypts mapped shares loves them too. Start with the inventory: every share, its purpose, its current permissions, and who actually uses it. Then apply the discipline: read access for the groups with a demonstrated need, write access only where the workflow requires it (the finance team writes to Finance; sales reads it), and no 'Everyone: Full Control' anywhere — that entry is a finding in every audit for a reason. Scoping follows the same per-user, per-group model as device policy: the policy engine evaluates the user and the machine, so the contractor on a loaner laptop gets different share access than the finance lead on their workstation. Worked example: the company's share redesign. Before: three shares, all 'Authenticated Users: Read/Write,' because it was easy in 2019. After: FINANCE — read for management and finance, write for finance only, reviewed quarterly. ENGINEERING — read for engineering and IT, write for engineering leads, reviewed quarterly. SHARED — read for all staff, write for department heads, with a 90-day review because shared folders accumulate sensitive data like sediment. The migration is staged: audit current usage first (who actually writes where — the logs will surprise you), announce the change, flip to the new permissions, and keep a two-week exception lane for the workflows you missed. The ransomware angle deserves emphasis: the M01 trace showed encryption spreading through mapped shares. Read-only-by-default shares do not just slow insiders; they contain the blast radius of a compromised standard user whose session can only read what the shares permit. Common gotcha: share permissions vs NTFS permissions confusion. Both layers must agree — a restrictive share with permissive NTFS (or vice versa) yields the permissive effective access. Document both, and verify effective access with a test account, not by reading the dialogs. Second gotcha: the 'temporary project share' that becomes permanent. Shares get owners and review dates like everything else in this course.  Remember that effective access is the intersection of both layers: the share permissions and the NTFS permissions must agree, or the more permissive one wins. Document both in the share record, and verify with a test account in each role — reading the dialogs is not verification. The classic failure is the 'secure' share with Everyone:Read at the share level and Users:Full at the NTFS level, or vice versa; the effective access is whatever the union permits. Your migration checklist should include an effective-access test per role per share, recorded in the ticket, before you declare the migration complete.

