Storage Control does not operate alone: it is one half of the data-protection pair, and the forensics it enables are what make the other half work. The pair is Storage Control plus Data Loss Prevention. Storage Control governs the path: may data move through this device, this share, this destination? It is identity-and-policy based — it knows who, what device, and where, but it does not inspect the content. DLP governs the content: is this data sensitive? It classifies — client lists, financial records, credentials, regulated data — and acts on the classification regardless of path. Together they cover the matrix: Storage Control stops the USB copy of anything, DLP stops the sensitive spreadsheet sent through an allowed channel. Neither alone is sufficient, and the operator should be able to explain the gap each leaves. The forensics side is where Storage Control pays for itself even when nothing is blocked. Every allowed and denied storage event is logged with the same schema: timestamp, user, machine, device class, device ID/serial, direction (read/write), file paths, byte counts, and the verdict. This log is the evidence chain for three questions investigators always ask. What left the machine? The write log with file paths and byte counts. When and by whom? The timestamp-user-machine triple. Was it authorized? The verdict field plus the exception register. Worked example: the post-incident timeline. A departing employee is suspected of taking client data. The investigator pulls the storage logs for the employee's last 30 days: USB writes (two blocked attempts, one allowed write to a registered drive — 400 MB, file paths listed), share access (read of the client folder, no anomalous volume), and cross-references the DLP log (no sensitive-content alerts on allowed channels). The timeline is built from logs, not from interviews — and the allowed write to the registered drive, with its file list, becomes the precise scope of the follow-up. Without the storage log, the investigation is guesswork; with it, it is accounting. Common gotcha: log retention shorter than the investigation horizon. Insider cases surface months later — if storage logs rotate after 30 days, the evidence is gone before the question is asked. Size retention to the threat model (a year is the common enterprise answer) and protect the log store itself. Second gotcha: collecting the logs but never reviewing the allowed events. Denied attempts get attention; allowed writes to registered devices are where the authorized-but-excessive data movement hides — sample them, especially around departures and role changes.

