Network Control is the endpoint firewall: the deny-by-default gate for packets, just as allowlisting is the gate for code. It manages dynamic access control lists on the machine itself — ports that open and close automatically based on policy and application need — and its two headline jobs are blocking lateral movement and blocking command-and-control callbacks. Understand first why the endpoint firewall is not redundant with the network firewall. The network firewall guards segments: it sees packets, applies rules per subnet, and stops at the perimeter. The endpoint firewall guards the machine: it sees the owning process behind every connection, applies policy per process identity, and follows the laptop to the café, the home office, and the customer site. That per-process visibility is the superpower: when the network firewall logs 'blocked outbound to evil.com,' you know a packet was stopped; when the endpoint firewall logs it, you know which process, launched by whom, from what parent, tried to call out — the exact context the M07 triage workflow needs. The baseline posture has four parts. Default-deny inbound: nothing may initiate connections to the machine unless policy explicitly permits it. Default-deny workstation-to-workstation: peers do not talk to peers — SMB, RDP, and WinRM between workstations are denied, which is the containment wall from the M01 assume-breach design. Scoped exceptions with justification: every allow names the ports, the remote scope, and the business reason. Default-deny egress for unknown processes: outbound connections are gated by process identity, so malware cannot simply phone home. Worked example: the café laptop. An employee works from a coffee shop on untrusted Wi-Fi. The network firewall is irrelevant — there is none. The endpoint firewall enforces the identical policy it enforces in the office: inbound denied, peer traffic denied, the browser allowed to 80/443, the unknown process's callback denied. The policy follows the machine because it lives on the machine. Common gotcha: 'we have a network firewall, so host firewall is redundant.' The network firewall never sees the laptop at the café, never knows which process opened the connection, and cannot enforce per-process identity. They are complementary layers, not substitutes. Second gotcha: managing endpoint firewall rules like network firewall rules — static, wide, forgotten. Endpoint rules should be dynamic and scoped: open when the approved application needs the port, closed when it does not, reviewed on the same cadence as the allowlist.  The endpoint firewall also feeds the EDR's network visibility: connection logs with process attribution are among the highest-value telemetry for threat hunting, because they show which process talked to where — the question every investigation asks first. Ensure the firewall logs are forwarded to the same store as the Detect alerts so the triage workflow sees them together: the blocked lateral attempt and the process tree in one view. A firewall whose logs stay local is a witness that never testifies.

