Static firewall rules are standing invitations: a port opened 'temporarily' in 2021 is still open, still probed, still waiting. Dynamic ACLs fix this by tying the port's state to policy and need — the port opens when the approved application requires it and closes when it does not, automatically. The concept is easiest to grasp through the contrast. Static rule: 'allow inbound TCP 8080 from anywhere, forever' — written for a demo, forgotten, discovered by a scanner within the hour, exploited within the month. Dynamic rule: 'when the approved backup agent (signed, allowlisted) starts its receive window, open TCP 8443 from the backup server's IP only; close it when the window ends' — the port exists for minutes per day, from one source, for one purpose. An attacker scanning at any other time finds nothing. The 'automatically' matters operationally: nobody files a ticket to open and close the port, so nobody forgets to close it. Worked example: the patch-management agent. Once a week it needs inbound connections from the management server to receive tasks. Static approach: port 8530 open from the management subnet permanently — a standing target that any compromised machine on that subnet can probe. Dynamic approach: the ACL opens 8530 from the single management server IP during the agent's maintenance window and closes it after. The weekly exposure drops from 168 hours to 2, from a subnet to a single IP. Multiply that across every service and the machine's attack surface becomes a flickering target instead of a lit runway. The design discipline: every allow rule states its trigger (what opens it), its scope (from whom), and its lifetime (when it closes). Rules without lifetimes are static rules with better marketing — audit for them specifically. Common gotcha: dynamic ACLs for user applications that 'need' inbound. Workstations almost never legitimately need inbound connections — the application claiming to need one is usually misconfigured (it should poll outbound) or is peer-to-peer software that does not belong on the workstation. Challenge every inbound request on a workstation; the answer is usually 'redesign the app flow,' not 'open the port.' Second gotcha: confusing dynamic with permissive. Dynamic does not mean 'opens whenever asked' — it means 'opens when policy's conditions are met.' The conditions are the control.  Give every rule a lifecycle, not just a creation. The rule record states the owner, the justification, the review date, and the condition under which it is removed (project end, decommission, migration complete). Review the rule set on the same cadence as the allowlist — quarterly at minimum — and expire rules whose justification has lapsed. Firewall rules, like allowlist entries, rot: the 'temporary' vendor access from last year's migration is still open, and the scanner will find it before you remember it.

