Firewall policy is the number-one suspect when 'the app worked yesterday,' so the troubleshooting discipline must be structured — random rule-toggling creates holes. The triage order is fixed. Step one: identify the blocked flow. Pull the firewall log for the timeframe: source, destination, port, protocol, direction, and — the endpoint firewall's gift — the owning process. Most 'mystery outages' end here: the log names the exact denied connection. Step two: check the owning process against policy. Is this process approved? Is it the legitimate application or something wearing its name? Verify identity (hash, certificate, path) before touching any rule — opening a port for malware because 'the app needed it' is the classic self-inflicted breach. Step three: verify the destination's legitimacy. Is this the vendor's real update endpoint or a lookalike? Check DNS, check the vendor's published endpoints, be suspicious of anything newly registered. Step four: write the scoped exception. The exception names the process, the ports, the destination scope, and the justification — and it goes above the deny rules, documented, with a review date. Never 'allow all outbound for the app to see if that fixes it' — that is not troubleshooting, it is surrender. Worked example: Monday 9 AM, the accounting package cannot reach its cloud service after a weekend policy push. Triage: the firewall log shows denied outbound TCP 443 from AcctApp.exe to 203.0.113.45. Process check: AcctApp.exe is allowlisted, signed by the vendor, parent chain clean. Destination check: 203.0.113.45 is in the vendor's published endpoint list (verified via the vendor's docs, not the user's word). Verdict: the weekend policy tightened egress without the vendor's endpoint range. Fix: scoped exception — AcctApp.exe may reach the vendor's published endpoint range on 443, documented with the ticket, review in 90 days. Total downtime: 20 minutes, because the triage order went straight to the evidence. Common gotcha: the 'temporary allow-any' that becomes permanent. Every troubleshooter is tempted; the discipline is writing the scoped rule even under pressure, because the emergency you fix today with a hole becomes the incident you investigate next quarter. Second gotcha: troubleshooting the firewall when the problem is DNS, the proxy, or the application itself. The firewall log is step one precisely because it tells you in seconds whether the firewall is even involved — if there is no denied flow, look elsewhere.  Record every exception in the change system with the same five fields as the allowlist: requester, justification, scope, expiry, approver. The firewall exception register is audited alongside the others, and 'temporary vendor access' without an expiry is the rule most likely to be found open two years later. When the auditor asks why port X is open to subnet Y, the register should answer in one lookup — that is the difference between a managed firewall and a collection of rules.

