ThreatLocker Detect is the module that turns prevention into intelligence: it alerts on the indicators of compromise that the other modules blocked. Every denied execution, every fenced child-process spawn, every blocked USB write and every denied lateral connection emits an alert carrying the full context — the process, its parent chain, the user, the machine, the timestamp, and exactly which policy said no. Understand why this inverts the traditional SOC experience. In a detection-first world (classic EDR), the sensor watches the attack run and tries to recognize malice in progress: behavioral analytics, machine-learning scores, threat-intel matches. The alert says, in effect, 'something looks 73% malicious, please investigate while it continues.' Analysts drown in probabilistic leads, most benign, and the true positives arrive while the damage is underway. In the prevention-first world, the enforcement already stopped the attempt — the alert says 'this specific action was attempted and denied,' which is a statement of fact, not a probability. A blocked child-process spawn from Word at 9:02 AM is not 73% suspicious; it is an attempted attack step, caught red-handed, with the evidence attached. This is why prevention-first alerts are higher fidelity: the enforcement decision pre-filters the noise. The benign application that behaves normally never triggers; the malicious step triggers by attempting the forbidden. Worked example: compare the two SOC mornings. Detection-first SOC: 400 alerts overnight — 380 benign (updaters, admin tools, user oddities scored as suspicious), 15 duplicates, 5 worth reading, 1 real. The analyst's morning is triage-as-archaeology, and the real one is buried. Prevention-first SOC: 12 alerts overnight — each a blocked action with full context. The analyst reads 12 factual events, clears 8 as benign-with-reason (stale hash rule, approved updater), and escalates 4 with evidence. Same company, same threats, radically different signal-to-noise — because the blocks did the filtering. Common gotcha: 'fewer alerts means less visibility.' The opposite: the prevented attempts are the highest-value visibility you can get — malice confirmed at the attempt stage, before damage. The SOC's job shifts from finding needles in haystacks to judging 12 needles, which is a job humans can actually do well. Second gotcha: treating Detect as 'just logging.' The alert pipeline — severity, routing, SLA, runbook — is what makes the blocks operationally useful. Blocks without triage are security theater with receipts.  Design the alert routing before the alerts arrive. Critical-severity alerts page the on-call analyst; high-severity alerts land in the priority queue with a one-hour triage SLA; everything else flows to the standard queue. Route by asset criticality too — the domain controller's medium is the kiosk's critical. And build the queue dashboards around the runbook's verdicts, not raw counts: 'alerts triaged to incident this week' is a security metric, 'total alerts' is just weather. The routing design is what keeps the 2 AM alert from waiting until 9 AM.

