Every alert gets the same five-input triage, in the same order, because consistency is what makes a SOC defensible and auditable. Input one: severity. The alert's initial severity comes from the policy that fired and the action blocked — a blocked unsigned execution in temp is higher severity than a blocked updater with a stale rule. Severity sets the SLA clock: critical starts triage in minutes, medium in hours. Input two: asset criticality. The same block on the domain controller and on a kiosk are different events — know your crown jewels (finance workstations, servers, executive machines, IT admin boxes) and weight accordingly. Input three: user and role context. Who was at the keyboard? The finance user's blocked macro at 9 AM during month-end reads differently than the same block on a service account at 3 AM. Check whether the user is where they should be, doing what they should do. Input four: the blocked action's details. Read the actual event: which process, which parent, what was attempted, what was the target, what arguments were passed? Encoded PowerShell arguments are a different universe from a missing file path. Input five: pattern history. Has this user, this machine, this process generated similar alerts? One blocked USB write is curiosity; three in a week across machines is intent. The verdict has three branches. Benign: the block was correct but harmless — stale rule, approved tool, user error. Action: fix the rule or educate the user, document, close. Policy violation without malice: the user tried to bypass policy for convenience. Action: document, retrain, check whether a broken legitimate workflow drove it. Incident: real attack attempt or suspicious pattern. Action: escalate per the runbook — isolate, preserve, investigate. Worked example: the 9:02 AM alert from M01. Severity: high (blocked child-process spawn, encoded args). Asset: accounting workstation (crown jewel — finance data). User: m.reyes, finance, at her desk at 9 AM (context fits). Action details: winword.exe → powershell.exe, encoded command line (malicious pattern). History: first alert for this user/machine. Verdict: incident — isolate ACCOUNTING-07, preserve the logs, escalate to IR. The triage took the five inputs in order and produced a defensible verdict in minutes. Common gotcha: verdict before context. Reading the alert title and deciding 'it's just the updater' without checking the arguments is how real intrusions get closed as benign. The workflow's order exists to prevent exactly this. Second gotcha: severity as destiny. Initial severity starts the clock; the triage determines the verdict. A 'medium' alert with a malicious pattern history becomes an incident — let the inputs move the verdict.

