Prevention-first versus detection-first is the architectural debate underneath every EDR conversation, and you should be able to argue it from the operator's chair. Detection-first (traditional EDR) assumes the attack will run: the sensor monitors process behavior, network flows, and file activity, and tries to recognize malice while it executes. Its strengths are real: it catches fileless attacks, it sees the techniques that prevention misses, and its telemetry is invaluable for hunting. Its structural weakness is time: recognition happens during or after the malicious action, so the SOC races the attacker — and the attacker only needs to be faster than the analyst's queue. Prevention-first (Detect's model) assumes the attempt can be stopped: the policy denies the malicious action before it executes, and the denial becomes the alert. Its strength is the timeline: the SOC starts at the attempt stage, with the attack already dead. Its honest limitation is coverage: prevention stops what policy forbids, so novel techniques outside the fenced behaviors still need detection — which is why the mature design is prevention-first with detection depth, not prevention-only. The dwell-time argument needs care: you will hear claims about average attacker dwell times, but do not quote numbers you cannot source. The structural argument needs no numbers: an alert at the attempt stage is earlier than an alert at the damage stage, by construction. Worked example: the same intrusion against both architectures. Detection-first: the attacker lands, the EDR scores the behavior, an alert fires mid-encryption, the analyst starts triage while files encrypt — the race is on. Prevention-first: the attacker's first malicious step (the child-process spawn) is denied at 9:02:14, the alert fires at 9:02:15 with the attack dead — the analyst triages a corpse, calmly, with full evidence. The incident still gets investigated (how did the document arrive? are there others?), but nobody is racing encryption. Common gotcha: presenting prevention-first as making detection obsolete. It does not — it changes detection's job from 'catch the attack in progress' to 'judge the attempts and hunt what policy missed.' The SOC still hunts; it just hunts from a position of already having stopped the bleeding. Second gotcha: assuming prevented means uninteresting. The blocked attempt is the highest-quality threat intelligence the company produces — it names the attacker's current tools, targets, and timing. Feed it to the threat-intel process, not just the ticket queue.  Close the loop between triage and prevention. Every incident verdict should produce at least one candidate policy improvement: a new ringfence behavior to deny, an allowlist rule to tighten, an egress destination to remove. Track these as follow-up tickets owned by the policy team, not as informal suggestions — the SOC sees the attacks, the policy team owns the gates, and the feedback ticket is the bridge. A SOC whose findings never change policy is an expensive observation deck.

