<text x="386.0" y="98" font-family="ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,SF Mono,Menlo,Consolas,monospace" font-size="12" fill="#f0f6fc" text-anchor="start" font-weight="400" opacity="1.0" class="a-node" style="animation-delay:2.45s">• Build a Detect alert runbook with…</text></svg><figcaption style="margin-top:.6em;color:#8b949e;font-size:.85em;text-align:center"><strong>Fig. IL29-M07-D4 — </strong>Certification exams test two things: what you can explain (left) and what you can do (right). This module&#x27;s objectives split that way — drill the left column for recall, and practice the right column hands-on under time pressure.</figcaption></figure>

Alert fatigue is the SOC's chronic illness: too many alerts, too little signal, analysts skimming, real incidents missed in the noise. Prevention-first architectures start with an advantage — the blocks pre-filter — but the advantage erodes without tuning discipline. The tuning program has four parts. Baselining: for the first 30-60 days, every alert type gets its benign rate measured — which alerts fire, how often, and what fraction prove benign. You cannot tune what you have not measured. Documented suppression: benign-by-pattern alerts get suppression rules — but every suppression is a written decision naming the pattern, the evidence it is benign, the owner, and the review date. 'We suppressed updater blocks' is blindness; 'we suppress blocks where the binary is vendor-signed, the hash matches the vendor's published value, and the parent is the approved updater — owner: SOC lead, review: quarterly' is tuning. Anomaly preservation: suppressions target known-benign patterns only — anything novel, anything from a new process, anything off-pattern still fires. The day the 'known-benign' updater behaves differently is the day you want the alert. Review cadence: suppressions expire and are re-justified on schedule, because vendors get compromised and benign patterns change. Worked example: the updater-block tuning. Week one: 40 alerts — 'blocked execution of vendor updater, hash mismatch.' Triage shows the vendor rotated their signing certificate; the binaries are legitimate. Suppression written: allow the new certificate for the updater product, scoped, owner named, 90-day review. Alerts drop to zero for this pattern — legitimately, because the policy now reflects reality. Contrast with the lazy version: 'suppress all updater alerts' — which would also suppress the trojaned updater next quarter. The case walkthrough that ties the module together: the full M01 attack as a Detect story. 9:02:14 — blocked child-process spawn (winword.exe → powershell.exe), severity high, full context attached. 9:02:15 — blocked file write to temp. 9:02:16 — blocked unknown execution. Three alerts, one incident, one timeline: the SOC sees the attacker's playbook in the order it was attempted, each step already dead. The analyst's job is verdict and scope (how did the document arrive? any other recipients?), not a race against encryption. That is the prevention-first SOC: fewer alerts, each factual, triaged at the attempt stage. Common gotcha: tuning by volume instead of by pattern. 'Reduce alerts by 50%' as a goal produces blind suppressions; 'every alert type has a measured benign rate and a documented disposition' produces a tuned SOC. Second gotcha: the hero analyst who 'just knows.' Institutionalize the knowledge in the runbook — heroes go on vacation, runbooks do not.

