Microsoft Defender is on every Windows endpoint you will manage, which makes its configuration one of the highest-leverage baselines in the environment — and one of the most commonly left to rot. Without centralized management, Defender configuration decays into folklore: real-time protection disabled on 'that one machine that was slow,' exclusions added by whoever had admin that day for reasons nobody remembers, cloud protection off because someone clicked through a prompt in 2022. Each machine becomes its own policy, the 'standard' exists only in a document nobody follows, and the auditor's sample inevitably finds the worst one. Configuration Manager fixes this by treating Defender settings as code: one baseline, defined centrally, pushed to every machine, with the actual configuration continuously compared against the intended. The baseline covers the protection stack. Real-time protection: the always-on guard that scans files on access — the setting that must never be off, because everything else assumes it is on. Cloud-delivered protection: unknown files get verdicts from Microsoft's cloud telemetry in seconds — the layer that covers what local signatures have never seen. Tamper protection: locks the settings themselves so malware (or a compromised admin) cannot silently disable the guard. Automatic sample submission and signature update cadence: the freshness of the intelligence. Exclusions: the deliberate blind spots, each justified (more on those below). Worked example: the 'slow machine' incident. A user complains their workstation is slow; a well-meaning tech disables real-time protection 'temporarily.' Six months later the machine is patient zero in a malware incident — and nobody knows protection was off because no central system compared actual to intended. With centralized management, the drift report flags the machine within the check cycle: 'WORKSTATION-14: real-time protection disabled, baseline requires enabled.' The operator re-pushes the baseline, investigates the performance complaint properly (which turns out to be a failing disk), and the hole closes in hours instead of being discovered during incident response. Common gotcha: assuming defaults are the baseline. Defender's out-of-box settings are reasonable but they are not your policy — cloud protection levels, sample submission consent, exclusion lists, and tamper protection all need deliberate decisions recorded in the baseline document. Second gotcha: managing the baseline but not the drift. A pushed policy that nobody verifies is a wish; the continuous comparison — intended vs actual, machine by machine — is what makes it a control.  Deliver the baseline through the same channel as the rest of your configuration management — Group Policy for domain-joined machines, the MDM for the mobile fleet — so the baseline inherits the existing change control, targeting, and reporting. Do not manage Defender through a side channel with its own console and its own process; the side channel becomes the thing everyone forgets during incidents. One configuration pipeline, one change record, one place to check what was pushed and when.

