<text x="136" y="370" font-family="ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,SF Mono,Menlo,Consolas,monospace" font-size="11" fill="#8b949e" text-anchor="start" font-weight="400" opacity="1.0" class="a-node" style="animation-delay:5.95s">Note 6 of 6</text></svg><figcaption style="margin-top:.6em;color:#8b949e;font-size:.85em;text-align:center"><strong>Fig. IL29-M08-D1 — </strong>The module&#x27;s full stack, top to bottom: start at Why Defender configuration needs… and work down to Rollout rings and the boring audit. Each layer builds on the one above it — skipping ahead leaves gaps.</figcaption></figure>

The baseline is a document before it is a deployment: every setting, its required value, and the one-line rationale. Write it like an auditor will read it, because one will. The protection core: real-time protection enabled — the always-on scanning at file access, the non-negotiable. Cloud-delivered protection set to the advanced level — unknown executables get cloud verdicts within seconds, which is the only scalable answer to never-before-seen files. Tamper protection enabled — settings locked against modification by malware or unauthorized admins; verify it is actually on, because it is the setting that protects the settings. Behavior monitoring enabled — the heuristic layer that catches malicious actions rather than just malicious files. Signature updates automatic on the fastest practical cadence — stale intelligence is a choice to be less protected. Sample submission configured per company policy — automatic submission of safe samples improves the collective verdicts; the policy decision is what may leave the building. Then the platform settings: scheduled scan cadence (the weekly full scan that catches what real-time missed), scan scope (all drives, including removable — the M05 device policy and the scan policy must agree), and threat-default-actions (what happens on detection: quarantine first, never 'allow'). Worked example: the baseline document excerpt. 'MAPSReporting = Advanced. Rationale: unknown files receive cloud verdicts in seconds; local-only verdicts miss zero-day samples. Risk accepted: file metadata leaves the machine per the data-handling policy §4.2.' Every setting gets this treatment — value plus rationale plus the risk decision. The document is versioned, change-controlled, and reviewed quarterly: baseline changes go through the same change process as firewall rules, because they are the same class of control. Common gotcha: the 'recommended' trap. Vendor 'recommended' settings are a starting point, not your baseline — your baseline reflects your threat model, your data-handling policy, and your operational capacity. If your SOC cannot handle the alert volume of the most aggressive heuristic level, the honest baseline is the level you will actually operate, documented as such. Second gotcha: baseline without exception process. Legitimate needs will conflict with the baseline (the developer tool that the heuristics hate) — the baseline document must name the exception process, or people will bypass the baseline silently. The exclusion discipline below is that process.  Watch the interplay between scan settings and exclusions: aggressive heuristic levels plus a broad exclusion list is the worst combination — maximum noise where you look, blindness where you do not. Tune them as a pair: set the protection level you can actually operate (alerts your SOC will triage, performance your users will tolerate), then keep the exclusion list as short as the narrowness ladder allows. The baseline document should record both decisions together with the reasoning, so the next operator understands the balance rather than inheriting half of it.

