<text x="360.0" y="130" font-family="ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,SF Mono,Menlo,Consolas,monospace" font-size="11" fill="#8b949e" text-anchor="middle" font-weight="400" opacity="1.0" class="a-node" style="animation-delay:1.75s">central</text>
<text x="360.0" y="154" font-family="ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,SF Mono,Menlo,Consolas,monospace" font-size="11" fill="#8b949e" text-anchor="middle" font-weight="400" opacity="1.0" class="a-node" style="animation-delay:2.10s">management</text>
<text x="360.0" y="178" font-family="ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,SF Mono,Menlo,Consolas,monospace" font-size="11" fill="#8b949e" text-anchor="middle" font-weight="400" opacity="1.0" class="a-node" style="animation-delay:2.45s">baseline</text></svg><figcaption style="margin-top:.6em;color:#8b949e;font-size:.85em;text-align:center"><strong>Fig. IL29-M08-D2 — </strong>The vocabulary this module installs — Defender, configuration, needs…. If you can define each term in one plain sentence, the module&#x27;s objectives are within reach.</figcaption></figure>

Exclusions are deliberate blind spots: places Defender is told not to look. Every exclusion ever added was added for a reason that felt good at the time — and every major Defender bypass story involves an exclusion. The discipline is therefore strict: no exclusion without the five fields. Requester: who asked, so there is a human attached. Justification: the specific technical reason — 'the build agent's output directory triggers heuristic false positives on every compile, verified with the vendor' is a justification; 'it was slow' is not. Scope: the narrowest possible — a process exclusion for the specific binary beats a path exclusion for the folder, which beats excluding the drive. Expiry and review: exclusions rot as software changes, so each gets a review date (90 days is the working default). Approver: someone who understands what 'Defender will not look here' means. The exclusion types map to the narrowness ladder. Process exclusions: Defender skips files opened by the named process — the narrowest, preferred for build tools and backup agents. Path exclusions: Defender skips the folder — broader, acceptable for vendor-verified application directories, never for user-writable locations. Extension exclusions: Defender skips file types — the broadest per-file-type, justified only for large non-executable data (and never for executables). File-type and the classic mistake: excluding .exe or script extensions 'for performance' is functionally disabling protection for the most dangerous files. Worked example: the build-server exclusion request. The dev team reports 40-minute builds with Defender scanning the output directory. The operator verifies: the directory contains only build artifacts, the build tool is allowlisted and signed, the false positives are documented. Exclusion written: process exclusion for buildtool.exe plus path exclusion for D:\build-output\ — scoped to the build servers, requested by the dev lead, approved by security, 90-day review, ticket BUILD-118. The builds drop to 12 minutes; the exclusion is fenced to machines whose entire purpose is building, and the review will catch it if the tool changes. Contrast with the hole version: 'exclude D:\ from scanning on all machines because someone's build was slow once.' Common gotcha: temp-folder exclusions. Excluding temp, downloads, or the user's profile from scanning blinds Defender exactly where malware lands — these are the last places that should ever be excluded, and any request for them is a red flag to investigate, not a ticket to approve. Second gotcha: exclusion without compensating control. A broad-but-necessary exclusion (the legacy app's folder) should trigger a question: what covers this blind spot instead? The allowlist still gates execution there; the ringfence still constrains behavior — document the compensating controls in the exclusion record.

