<text x="386.0" y="124" font-family="ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,SF Mono,Menlo,Consolas,monospace" font-size="12" fill="#f0f6fc" text-anchor="start" font-weight="400" opacity="1.0" class="a-node" style="animation-delay:2.45s">• Detect and remediate configuration…</text></svg><figcaption style="margin-top:.6em;color:#8b949e;font-size:.85em;text-align:center"><strong>Fig. IL29-M08-D4 — </strong>Certification exams test two things: what you can explain (left) and what you can do (right). This module&#x27;s objectives split that way — drill the left column for recall, and practice the right column hands-on under time pressure.</figcaption></figure>

The rollout and the audit are where this module meets the real world. Rollout first: a Defender baseline change is a change to the security posture of every machine, so it gets the staged discipline. Ring zero: the security team's own machines — if the baseline breaks the people who wrote it, better to learn at 10 AM than company-wide at 9. Ring one: IT and a pilot department, one week, with explicit monitoring — detection counts, block rates, helpdesk tickets mentioning slowness or blocks, and the drift report confirming the baseline actually applied. Ring two: the rest, in waves, with the rollback trigger armed: a defined threshold of legitimate-impact tickets pauses the rollout for review. The emergency brake is the baseline versioning: every push is versioned, so rollback is 're-push the previous version,' not archaeology. Then the audit story, because this module exists partly to make audits boring. The auditor asks: 'prove consistent antivirus configuration across the estate.' The unmanaged answer is a scramble — screenshots from three machines, a policy document of uncertain age, and hope. The managed answer is two artifacts: the versioned baseline document (every setting, value, rationale, change history) and the latest drift report (intended vs actual, machine by machine, divergences remediated with tickets). The auditor samples five machines; all five match the baseline; the two divergences from last month show remediation tickets. That is a control, demonstrated. Worked example: the quarterly review meeting. The operator brings three things: the drift trend (divergences per week, falling since the OU fix), the exclusion register (12 active exclusions, 3 due for review, 2 removed last quarter), and the proposed baseline change (stricter cloud-protection level, with pilot results from ring zero showing no legitimate impact). The meeting takes 20 minutes because the artifacts do the talking. This is what 'managed' looks like from the outside: boring, evidenced, continuous. Common gotcha: the big-bang baseline push. 'We're pushing the new baseline to all 200 machines Friday at 5' is how you spend the weekend rolling back. Staged, monitored, reversible — the discipline is the same in M02, M03, and here, because the failure mode (breaking the business at scale) is the same. Second gotcha: audit theater — a beautiful baseline document with no drift detection behind it. The document claims; the drift report proves. Bring both, always.  Run the quarterly review as a business meeting, not a technical deep dive. The operator brings the drift trend, the exclusion register with upcoming reviews, and one proposed change with pilot data — the leadership brings the risk appetite. Twenty minutes, three artifacts, decisions recorded. This cadence is what keeps the baseline a living standard instead of a document: the review is where stale exclusions die, where drift patterns get resourced, and where the next quarter's hardening is agreed. Boring, regular, effective.

