An origin is the triple (scheme, host, port). https://bank.example:443 and https://bank.example:8443 are different origins; https://bank.example and http://bank.example are different origins; https://app.bank.example and https://bank.example are different origins. The same-origin policy (SOP) is the browser's core isolation rule: a script running in one origin cannot read the DOM, cookies, localStorage, or fetch responses of another origin. Without it, any page you visit could read your webmail.

Worked example: you are logged into webmail at https://mail.example. You open an attacker's page at https://evil.example in another tab. The attacker's JavaScript runs fetch('https://mail.example/inbox'). The browser sends the request (your mail cookies go along, because cookies attach by destination domain), but the SOP blocks the evil page from reading the response. The attacker's script gets a network error, not your inbox. Reading is blocked; sending is not. That asymmetry is the source of half of web security: CSRF exploits the sending side, XSS defeats the reading side by running inside the victim origin.

What the SOP does not do: it does not stop you from embedding cross-origin content (an <img>, <script src>, or <iframe> can load cross-origin resources; the embedding page just cannot read their internals). It does not protect against the user being tricked. And it has deliberate, controlled holes: CORS lets a server opt into cross-origin reads via Access-Control-Allow-Origin headers, and postMessage lets cooperating frames exchange messages with explicit origin checks. Common operator gotcha: CORS misconfiguration such as Access-Control-Allow-Origin: * combined with Access-Control-Allow-Credentials: true hands cross-origin readers the keys — when you audit an app, that header pair is an instant finding.

A frequent point of confusion is how CORS preflights fit in. For 'non-simple' cross-origin requests (custom headers, JSON content types), the browser first sends an OPTIONS preflight asking the server 'may I?' — only if the server answers with the right Access-Control-Allow-* headers does the real request go. Simple form POSTs skip preflight for historical reasons, which is exactly why CSRF works: the 'simple request' legacy path predates the SOP's stricter instincts. As an operator, test your CORS policy deliberately: from an origin you control, attempt credentialed fetches against your APIs and confirm the responses you expect to be private are not readable. And note the deprecated document.domain relaxation — older code set document.domain to merge subdomains into one origin; modern browsers are removing it because it punched holes in the SOP. If you inherit code using it, that's a finding. As a concrete exercise, open two local origins in separate tabs and attempt cross-origin reads in the console — watching the SOP deny them builds the instinct faster than any diagram.

