<text x="360.0" y="106" font-family="ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,SF Mono,Menlo,Consolas,monospace" font-size="11" fill="#8b949e" text-anchor="middle" font-weight="400" opacity="1.0" class="a-node" style="animation-delay:1.40s">12 flashcards — spaced recall of key terms</text>
<text x="360.0" y="130" font-family="ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,SF Mono,Menlo,Consolas,monospace" font-size="11" fill="#8b949e" text-anchor="middle" font-weight="400" opacity="1.0" class="a-node" style="animation-delay:1.75s">Mock task — full exam rehearsal</text>
<text x="360.0" y="154" font-family="ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,SF Mono,Menlo,Consolas,monospace" font-size="11" fill="#8b949e" text-anchor="middle" font-weight="400" opacity="1.0" class="a-node" style="animation-delay:2.10s">Hands-on lab — guided real-tool practice</text></svg><figcaption style="margin-top:.6em;color:#8b949e;font-size:.85em;text-align:center"><strong>Fig. IL29-M28-D4 — </strong>How this module checks mastery: 5 practice channels. Reading the notes is the input — these are the outputs that prove it stuck.</figcaption></figure>

Boolean algebra is the algebra of true and false — the same laws you met as logic in M28, now as equations you compute with. The variables take values 1 (true) and 0 (false); the operators are ∧ (AND), ∨ (OR), ¬ (NOT). The laws, each verifiable by a four-row truth table: Identity — A∧1=A, A∨0=A (1 is the AND-identity, 0 the OR-identity). Complement — A∧¬A=0, A∨¬A=1 (a thing and its negation are never both true, always one true). Idempotent — A∧A=A, A∨A=A (repeating a condition changes nothing — unlike arithmetic). Commutative and associative — order and grouping don't matter, so you can rearrange freely. Distributive — A∧(B∨C)=(A∧B)∨(A∧C), and the less familiar A∨(B∧C)=(A∨B)∧(A∨C) (OR distributes over AND too — this one surprises people). Absorption — A∨(A∧B)=A and A∧(A∨B)=A: the bigger term swallows the smaller. Worked verification of absorption, row by row for A∨(A∧B)=A: if A=1, left side is 1∨(anything)=1=A. If A=0, left side is 0∨(0∧B)=0∨0=0=A. Both rows match — proved. Why operators care: these laws are the simplification engine for every compound condition you write — firewall rules, detection logic, access checks. Worked simplification: (A∧B)∨(A∧¬B). Distribute A out: A∧(B∨¬B). Complement: B∨¬B=1. Identity: A∧1=A. Three steps collapse a four-literal expression to one variable — and the simplified condition is cheaper to evaluate and easier to audit. Second worked example: (admin ∧ vpn) ∨ (admin ∧ ¬vpn) ∨ (¬admin ∧ vpn) — 'admins anywhere plus VPN users.' Group the first two: admin∧(vpn∨¬vpn)=admin. Left with admin ∨ (¬admin ∧ vpn); absorption's dual (A∨(¬A∧B)=A∨B): = admin ∨ vpn. The policy means 'admins or VPN users' — the simplification reveals intent the original obscured. Common mistake: treating Boolean algebra like arithmetic — A∨A is A, not 2A; there is no subtraction; and distribution goes both directions. Simplify conditions aggressively: every redundant literal is a future misreading.

There is a meta-pattern worth naming: duality. Every Boolean law has a dual obtained by swapping AND with OR and 0 with 1 — absorption's A OR (A AND B) = A duals to A AND (A OR B) = A, and De Morgan's laws are duals of each other. When you prove or learn one form, you get the other free. Use duality as a bug detector: if a simplification looks right in one form, check its dual — if the dual looks wrong, recheck the original. And treat the laws as refactoring rules for conditions, the way you would refactor code: the goal is always fewer literals and clearer intent. A condition like (isAdmin AND inVPN) OR (isAdmin AND NOT inVPN) OR (NOT isAdmin AND inVPN) should trigger the same instinct as a duplicated code block — simplify to isAdmin OR inVPN and delete the cleverness. Reviewers should flag unsimplified conditions the way they flag duplicated code: not because they are wrong, but because the next person to touch them will make them wrong. Simplicity is not aesthetic here; it is the difference between a condition you can audit and one you merely hope about.

