<text x="636.0" y="84" font-family="ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,SF Mono,Menlo,Consolas,monospace" font-size="14" fill="#f0f6fc" text-anchor="middle" font-weight="700" opacity="1.0" class="a-node" style="animation-delay:3.50s">Masks and…</text>
<text x="636.0" y="104" font-family="ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,SF Mono,Menlo,Consolas,monospace" font-size="11" fill="#8b949e" text-anchor="middle" font-weight="400" opacity="1.0" class="a-node" style="animation-delay:3.85s">Note 4</text></svg><figcaption style="margin-top:.6em;color:#8b949e;font-size:.85em;text-align:center"><strong>Fig. IL29-M29-D1 — </strong>Read the notes in order — Boolean algebra laws: the… lays the foundation and Masks and flags: permission… brings it together. Each note assumes the vocabulary of the ones before it.</figcaption></figure>

De Morgan's laws return here in algebraic dress — you proved them by truth table in M28; now wield them as rewrite rules for simplifying and negating real expressions. ¬(A∧B) = ¬A∨¬B. ¬(A∨B) = ¬A∧¬B. The operational reading: to negate an AND, OR the negations; to negate an OR, AND the negations. The connective always flips — the single fact that prevents an entire class of access-control bugs. Worked simplification 1 — push negation inward: ¬((A∨B)∧¬C). The outer ¬ sits over a ∧, so flip to ∨ with both sides negated: ¬(A∨B) ∨ ¬¬C. Push into the first: (¬A∧¬B) ∨ C (double negation ¬¬C=C). Final: (¬A∧¬B)∨C — negation now touches only literals, the canonical 'negation normal form' that detection engines and policy compilers actually want. Worked simplification 2 — the access-control bug shape: a policy intends 'deny unless (admin AND from-VPN)', i.e., allow = A∧V, deny = ¬(A∧V). De Morgan: deny = ¬A∨¬V — deny anyone who is not an admin OR not on the VPN. The buggy version a tired engineer writes: ¬A∧¬V — deny only people who are both non-admin AND off-VPN, which admits non-admins connecting via VPN. One flipped connective, one authentication bypass. Worked simplification 3 — combine with absorption: ¬(¬(A∨B)∨¬(A∧B))... take it in stages. Inner: ¬(A∨B)=¬A∧¬B; ¬(A∧B)=¬A∨¬B. So ¬((¬A∧¬B)∨(¬A∨¬B)). The inner disjunction: (¬A∧¬B)∨¬A∨¬B — absorption twice (X∨(X∧Y)=X with X=¬A, then X=¬B): = ¬A∨¬B. Then outer ¬: ¬(¬A∨¬B) = A∧B. The whole expression collapses to A∧B — verify with a truth table if you doubt it. That is the power of the toolkit: mechanical simplification of expressions no one could read directly. Common mistake: 'distributing' the ¬ without flipping (¬(A∧B)→¬A∧¬B). Say it as a mantra while you work: negation flips the connective. Every time.

Detection engineers meet De Morgan in SIEM rules constantly. A rule like 'alert when NOT (known_good_process AND expected_parent)' must be written as 'alert when (NOT known_good_process) OR (NOT expected_parent)' — and the number of detection rules that get this wrong in production is nonzero and embarrassing. The failure mode is silent: the buggy AND version under-alerts (it only fires when both are anomalous), so nobody notices the gap until the incident review asks why the rule did not fire. Defensive practice: write detection conditions in negation normal form (negations pushed to literals) as a team standard, and add a review checklist item for every NOT spanning a compound. Better yet, express the positive case and negate once at the end: define is_expected = known_good AND expected_parent, then alert on NOT is_expected — one negation, no distribution, no De Morgan bug possible. The broader lesson from this note: every manual application of De Morgan is a chance to err, so structure conditions to minimize the applications. Let the algebra do the work once, at design time, not in the analyst's head at 3 AM.

