CompTIA Security+ SY0-701 is the current version of the exam. Its published format: up to 90 questions, 90 minutes, multiple-choice plus performance-based questions (PBQs), and a passing score of 750 on a 100-900 scale. The five domains and their weights are: 1.0 General Security Concepts at 12 percent, 2.0 Threats, Vulnerabilities, and Mitigations at 22 percent, 3.0 Security Architecture at 18 percent, 4.0 Security Operations at 28 percent, and 5.0 Security Program Management and Oversight at 20 percent. Add the weights: 12 + 22 + 18 + 28 + 20 = 100. That single arithmetic check tells you the blueprint is a zero-sum budget of your study time: Security Operations alone is worth more than domains 1 and 3 combined. Worked example — pacing math. Ninety minutes for up to 90 questions means a nominal pace of one question per minute. But PBQs (drag-and-drop network diagrams, firewall rule matching, log triage simulations) can consume five to eight minutes each, and there are typically a few of them. The working method: on your first pass, answer everything you can solve in under a minute, flag PBQs and any question needing more than ninety seconds, and finish the pass with about 55-60 minutes elapsed. Your second pass then owns the flagged set with the remaining time. Never leave a question unanswered — there is no penalty for a wrong answer, and a blank is a guaranteed zero. Common mistake: treating 750/900 as 83 percent and therefore deciding you need to master 83 percent of everything. The scale is not a raw percentage; it is a scaled score, and CompTIA does not publish the raw-to-scaled conversion. The practical takeaway is simpler: you need a comfortable majority of questions correct, and because questions are distributed roughly by domain weight, your heaviest domain (Security Operations, 28 percent) is also where the most points live. A second gotcha: people walk in having memorized definitions but having never practiced PBQ-style tasks. CompTIA's performance-based questions test whether you can DO the thing — match the log to the attack, order the incident-response steps, place the control in the architecture. Every lab in IL-29 is practice for that exact skill. Your exam-day checklist therefore has two halves: know the five domains cold (definitions and classifications), and be able to perform the operational tasks in domains 2 through 4 under time pressure. One more exam-day mechanic: CompTIA randomizes question order, so your first pass is really a hunt for quick wins across the whole exam. Practice this on every timed set - never answer sequentially out of habit. And remember that PBQs are often front-loaded; if the first screen is a simulation, breathe, flag it, and start with question two. The points are identical wherever they sit, and confidence compounds: banking ten quick wins in the first fifteen minutes changes how you handle the hard questions later.

