<text x="636.0" y="104" font-family="ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,SF Mono,Menlo,Consolas,monospace" font-size="11" fill="#8b949e" text-anchor="middle" font-weight="400" opacity="1.0" class="a-node" style="animation-delay:3.85s">Note 4</text></svg><figcaption style="margin-top:.6em;color:#8b949e;font-size:.85em;text-align:center"><strong>Fig. IL29-M33-D1 — </strong>Read the notes in order — The SY0-701 exam blueprint:… lays the foundation and Domain 3.0 — Security… brings it together. Each note assumes the vocabulary of the ones before it.</figcaption></figure>

Domain 1.0 is the vocabulary layer every other domain assumes. Its core ideas: the CIA triad (confidentiality, integrity, availability) and its extended family (authentication, non-repudiation); the AAA framework (authentication, authorization, accounting); zero trust principles (never trust, always verify; assume breach; verify explicitly; least privilege; microsegmentation); physical security controls; deception and disruption technology (honeypots, honeynets, honeytokens); and control types and categories. Control types are the exam's favorite classification game. Types: technical (implemented in systems — firewalls, encryption, MFA), managerial (policies, procedures, risk assessments), operational (human processes — guards, awareness training, background checks), and physical (locks, cameras, mantraps). Categories: preventive (stop the event — firewall rules, least privilege), detective (find the event — IDS, log monitoring, audits), corrective (fix after the event — backups, patches, incident response), compensating (alternative when the ideal control is infeasible — a camera where a guard cannot be posted), and directive (steer behavior — policies, signage). Worked example. Scenario: 'A company places a honeypot server on its network to attract and study attackers.' Classification: this is a deception technology, and as a control it is technical (a system) and detective (it reveals attacker activity), deployed at the network/host layer. Second scenario: 'An organization requires security awareness training for all new hires.' Classification: operational control, directive category — it shapes human behavior rather than blocking an attack directly. Common mistake: calling a honeypot a preventive control. It does not prevent the initial intrusion; it detects and studies it, so it is detective. Another classic: confusing compensating with corrective. A compensating control is a substitute adopted because the primary control cannot be implemented; a corrective control restores normal operations after an incident. Zero trust appears here as a concept and then haunts the entire exam: every question about network design, access control, or endpoint policy is secretly asking whether you applied never-trust-always-verify and least privilege. When you read any SY0-701 question, first ask which control type and category is in play — half the distractors die at that step. A third worked example ties it together. Scenario: 'A company stores backups offline in a fireproof safe and tests restores quarterly.' Classification: the offline backup is a corrective control (it restores operations after an incident), implemented technically and physically, and the quarterly test is a managerial detective control (it verifies the corrective control works). Exam stems often bundle two controls - classify each separately and you will not be fooled. Also drill the zero-trust vocabulary until it is boring: 'never trust, always verify' is the phrase the exam wants, and every scenario about implicit trust (VPN users treated as safe, internal traffic uninspected) is a zero-trust violation. When a question asks for the BEST long-term fix for lateral movement after a phishing breach, the zero-trust answer - microsegmentation plus least privilege - beats 'a bigger firewall' every time.

