<text x="360.0" y="178" font-family="ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,SF Mono,Menlo,Consolas,monospace" font-size="11" fill="#8b949e" text-anchor="middle" font-weight="400" opacity="1.0" class="a-node" style="animation-delay:2.45s">Domain</text></svg><figcaption style="margin-top:.6em;color:#8b949e;font-size:.85em;text-align:center"><strong>Fig. IL29-M33-D2 — </strong>The vocabulary this module installs — exam, blueprint, format…. If you can define each term in one plain sentence, the module&#x27;s objectives are within reach.</figcaption></figure>

This is the second-heaviest domain and the one most people find intuitive, because it is the attacker-and-weakness catalog. It covers threat actors (their motivations and resources — nation-state, hacktivist, insider threat, organized crime, shadow IT as an internal risk), attack vectors and attack surfaces, malware families (ransomware, trojans, worms, rootkits, keyloggers, spyware, logic bombs, RATs), social engineering (phishing, spear phishing, whaling, vishing, smishing, pretexting, baiting, watering-hole attacks), vulnerability types (zero-day, misconfigurations, weak defaults, unpatched software, buffer overflows, race conditions, memory injection), and mitigation techniques (patching, hardening, segmentation, encryption, least privilege, secure baselines). Worked example — threat classification. Scenario: 'An attacker calls the help desk claiming to be the CFO, urgently requesting a password reset for the finance share.' This is pretexting (a fabricated scenario to manipulate) delivered via vishing (voice phishing). The mitigation stack: verify identity through an independent channel (callback to a known number), enforce a password-reset workflow that does not rely on caller claims, and train help-desk staff on social engineering — which ties back to Domain 1's operational/directive controls. Second worked example — vulnerability triage. 'A public-facing web server runs a version with a known remote-code-execution flaw; the vendor released a patch yesterday; the server cannot go down during business hours.' The vulnerability is an unpatched known flaw (not a zero-day — a patch exists). Mitigation: schedule an emergency change window, and as an interim compensating control, put the server behind a WAF rule blocking the exploit pattern and restrict its network reachability (segmentation). Common mistakes: calling every phishing email 'spear phishing' — spear phishing is targeted at a specific person or role; a mass credential-harvest email is just phishing. Calling a trojan a virus — viruses self-replicate by attaching to host files, worms self-propagate across networks, trojans disguise themselves as legitimate software and do not self-replicate. On exam day, underline the propagation mechanism in the question stem; it is almost always the discriminator between malware answers. A third worked example - attack surface reasoning. Scenario: 'An attacker scans the public IP range, finds an exposed RDP port, and brute-forces a weak password.' The attack vector is the exposed RDP service (attack surface: internet-facing remote access); the vulnerability is the weak password plus the exposed service; the mitigation stack is layered - remove RDP from the internet (attack-surface reduction), enforce MFA and account lockout (credential hardening), and put the service behind a VPN or jump host (network control). Exam questions love asking for the BEST mitigation, and the best answer is usually the one that shrinks the attack surface rather than the one that polices it. Common trap: choosing 'install antivirus' for a network-exposure problem - the vector is the exposure, so the fix must address the exposure. Another: confusing a vulnerability (the weakness) with a threat (the actor or event that exploits it). The weak password is the vulnerability; the brute-forcing attacker is the threat; together they create risk. Keep those three nouns straight and Domain 2.0 scenario stems become classification exercises.

