Here is how this course maps to the five domains, so you can study with a plan instead of a pile. Domain 1.0 (General Security Concepts, 12%): lean on IL-29's zero-trust foundations, control-type drills, and crypto/prereq-math modules — the vocabulary is already in your notes. Domain 2.0 (Threats/Vulnerabilities/Mitigations, 22%): use the threat-modeling, malware-analysis, and MITRE ATT&CK modules; build a personal malware-family flashcard set until you can name the propagation mechanism of each family in under five seconds. Domain 3.0 (Security Architecture, 18%): use the network-security, cloud-security, and secure-architecture modules; drill protocol-port pairs and the shared responsibility model until both are reflex. Domain 4.0 (Security Operations, 28%): this is your heaviest investment — the SIEM/log-analysis, vulnerability-management, incident-response, and forensics modules, plus every hands-on lab, because this domain tests doing, not reciting. Domain 5.0 (Program Management, 20%): use the governance/risk/compliance modules; practice risk calculations and framework-matching until the vocabulary is boring. Worked example — a four-week schedule. Week 1: domains 1 and 5 (concepts + governance, lighter reading, build your flashcard decks). Week 2: domain 2 (threats — heavy memorization, daily 20-minute malware/social-engineering drills). Week 3: domain 3 (architecture — whiteboard network designs, protocol-port drills). Week 4: domain 4 (operations — full labs, timed log-triage sets, incident-response ordering drills). Every week ends with a mixed 30-question set; the final three days are full timed runs. The timed practice gate is your go/no-go decision. Build a 90-question mixed set (or two 45-question halves), enforce 90 minutes with no pauses, score it, and break the score down by domain. Gate rule: overall scaled-equivalent at least 80 percent correct AND no domain below 65 percent correct. If Security Operations is at 58 percent, you do not schedule the exam — you do another week of domain 4 labs. Operator gotcha: most people fail practice gates on pacing, not knowledge — they spend six minutes on one PBQ-style scenario and then rush twenty questions. Practice the flag-and-return discipline from note 1 on every timed run until it is automatic. Schedule the real exam only after two consecutive gate passes on fresh question sets. One more planning tool: the error log. After every practice set, record each miss in four buckets - knowledge gap (never learned it), misread stem (knew it, rushed it), pacing casualty (ran out of time), distractor trap (fell for the tempting wrong answer). After three sets, the buckets reveal your real enemy: knowledge gaps need study blocks, misreads need slower first-pass discipline, pacing casualties need stricter flag-and-return, distractor traps need the 'what is the question REALLY asking' habit. Most candidates discover their enemy is not knowledge but process - and process is fixable in days, not weeks. Review the log before each new set; watching the same trap catch you twice is the only unforgivable error in this plan.

