CompTIA CySA+ CS0-003 is the current version of the exam. Its published format: a maximum of 85 questions, multiple-choice plus performance-based questions, 165 minutes, passing score 750/900. The four domains: 1.0 Security Operations at 33 percent, 2.0 Vulnerability Management at 30 percent, 3.0 Incident Response Management at 20 percent, and 4.0 Reporting and Communication at 17 percent. Check the arithmetic: 33 + 30 + 20 + 17 = 100. Notice the shape of this exam compared with Security+: CySA+ is almost two-thirds operations and vulnerability work (63 percent combined). It assumes you already know the Security+ vocabulary and tests whether you can do analyst work — read logs, prioritize findings, run an incident, and write the report. Worked example — pacing. One hundred sixty-five minutes over a maximum of 85 questions is roughly two minutes per question, more generous than Security+. But the performance-based questions here are heavier: analyzing a log bundle, tuning a correlation rule, ordering an incident-response workflow, or building a vulnerability-prioritization matrix can take eight to twelve minutes each. Apply the same flag-and-return discipline: first pass for everything answerable in under two minutes, second pass for the flagged PBQs, never leave blanks. A common mistake is treating CySA+ as 'Security+ part 2' and studying more definitions. The definitions are prerequisites; the exam tests judgment. When you read a stem, ask: what would an analyst do next in the SOC? The answer choices that describe analyst actions (tune the rule, isolate the host, prioritize by exploitability) beat the ones that describe memorized facts. Second gotcha: performance-based questions often have multiple defensible answers but one best action — the exam rewards the action that matches the phase of work you are in. If you are in detection, you investigate; if you are in containment, you isolate; if the incident is over, you write the lessons learned. Matching your action to the phase is the master key to Domain 3.0 questions. A second pacing scenario, because CySA+ PBQs are the real time sink. Suppose the exam serves you 80 multiple-choice and 5 PBQs. At two minutes each, the multiple-choice costs 160 minutes - already over budget. That arithmetic is why flag-and-return is non-negotiable: answer the 60 multiple-choice you know in about 70 minutes, bank them, then spend the remaining 95 minutes on the 20 harder multiple-choice plus the 5 PBQs. The candidates who fail CySA+ on time usually did the opposite - they wrestled each PBQ in order and never reached the last 25 questions. Also note the psychological shape: CySA+ stems are longer than Security+'s, with log snippets and scenario setups. Train yourself to read the actual question FIRST (the last sentence), then read the stem for exactly what it asks. Analysts who read the whole log bundle before knowing the question burn ten minutes on data they never needed - the same discipline as a real SOC queue, and the exam is deliberately testing it.

