At 33 percent, Security Operations is the heart of CySA+. It covers SOC workflow, SIEM engineering (log sources, correlation rules, alert tuning), threat hunting (hypothesis-driven searching for undetected threats), detection types and the confusion matrix of detection (true positive, false positive, true negative, false negative), and continuous monitoring. Worked example — the detection matrix. A SIEM rule watches for impossible-travel logins. Case A: an attacker logs in from two countries minutes apart and the rule fires — true positive (real attack, alert fired). Case B: an employee uses a VPN endpoint in another country and the rule fires — false positive (no attack, alert fired). Case C: an attacker uses a residential proxy in the same city and the rule stays silent — false negative (real attack, no alert). Case D: normal logins, no alert — true negative. The false negative is the dangerous one; the false positive is the expensive one (analyst time). Tuning a rule is the eternal trade: tighten it and false positives drop but false negatives rise; loosen it and you catch more at the cost of noise. Second worked example — threat hunting. An intel report describes a new ransomware group using scheduled tasks named like system utilities. No alerts have fired. The analyst hypothesizes the group may already be present, queries EDR data for scheduled-task creations with mismatched names, and finds three. That is threat hunting: you start from a hypothesis and go looking instead of waiting for alerts. Common mistakes: confusing threat hunting (proactive, hypothesis-driven) with alert triage (reactive, queue-driven); and answering SIEM questions with 'buy more log sources' when the scenario shows the data already exists — the analyst's job is usually correlation and tuning, not procurement. Also: continuous monitoring is not a tool, it is the practice — the SOC never stops watching, and the exam treats monitoring gaps as findings. A third worked example - alert tuning as an exam scenario. 'A correlation rule fires 300 times daily on legitimate vulnerability-scanner traffic, and analysts now ignore the rule entirely.' The diagnosis: a false-positive flood causing alert fatigue - which is operationally equivalent to a false negative, because real attacks hide in the ignored noise. The fix: tune the rule to exclude the scanner's IP range or require a second indicator (e.g., scanner traffic PLUS an unexpected process launch), then verify the tuned rule still fires on a known-bad test case. The exam tests this as a process question: the wrong answers are 'disable the rule' (creates a real blind spot) and 'hire more analysts' (scales the noise). The right answer is always tuning with verification. Common mistake: confusing continuous monitoring with a tool purchase. Monitoring is the practice - log sources feeding correlation feeding triage, around the clock. The exam treats 'we bought a SIEM but nobody watches the queue on weekends' as a monitoring failure, and the finding is process, not product.

