At 30 percent, Vulnerability Management is the second pillar: find weaknesses, prioritize them, remediate or compensate, and verify. It covers scanning (credentialed vs. non-credentialed, agent-based vs. network-based), vulnerability classification and scoring concepts, prioritization, remediation workflows, and compensating controls when patching is infeasible. Worked example — prioritization. Three findings: (A) a critical remote-code-execution flaw with a public exploit, on the internet-facing web server; (B) a critical flaw with no public exploit, on an isolated test VM; (C) a medium flaw on the domain controller. The exam's prioritization formula is exploitability x exposure x asset criticality. A wins outright: weaponized, exposed, production. Between B and C: C — the domain controller's criticality elevates a medium flaw above an isolated theoretical critical. So the order is A, C, B. Memorize that logic; it answers half the Domain 2.0 scenario questions. Second worked example — the remediation workflow. A scan finds an unpatched service on 40 workstations. The analyst validates the finding (no false positive), prioritizes (widespread, exploitable, medium asset value), opens a change ticket, coordinates a maintenance window, deploys the patch to a pilot group, verifies, rolls out fully, then rescans to confirm closure. Skipping the rescan is the classic operational failure — unverified remediation is an open finding. Common mistakes: treating a vulnerability scan as a penetration test (scanning finds weaknesses without exploiting; pen testing is an authorized attack that exploits within scope); calling every unpatched system a 'zero-day' (zero-day means no patch exists — if a patch exists and was not applied, it is an unpatched known vulnerability, and the fix is process, not research); and forgetting compensating controls — when the vendor patch is 30 days out, segmentation, WAF rules, and disabling the service are the correct interim answers, not 'wait for the patch.' A third worked example - the scan-types discriminator the exam loves. 'The team needs a complete inventory of missing patches on 500 workstations, including registry-level detail.' The answer is a credentialed scan: with valid credentials, the scanner reads the registry and patch history directly - accurate and fast. A non-credentialed scan would only infer from network banners, missing half the findings. Contrast: 'The team wants to see what an external attacker sees.' That is a non-credentialed, external scan - no credentials, attacker's view. The exam pairs these deliberately; match the scan type to the goal. A second trap: scan timing. Credentialed scans during business hours can disrupt operations - the analyst's job includes scheduling, change control, and stakeholder notice, which is why vulnerability management is a program, not a button. And remember the lifecycle loop: scan, validate (kill false positives before you alarm anyone), prioritize (exploitability x exposure x criticality), remediate (change-controlled), rescan (verify closure). The exam's favorite wrong answer is any option that skips validation or the rescan - unverified findings waste everyone's time, and unverified fixes are open findings.

