IL-29 is built for exactly this exam's shape, so the mapping is direct. Domain 1.0 (Security Operations, 33%): use the SIEM, log-analysis, detection-engineering, and threat-hunting modules — plus every drill that asks you to read a log and name the attack. Your daily practice should be 20 minutes of log-triage sets; pattern recognition is a muscle. Domain 2.0 (Vulnerability Management, 30%): use the vulnerability-scanning, prioritization, and patch-management modules; run the prioritization formula (exploitability x exposure x asset criticality) on every scenario until it is reflex. Domain 3.0 (Incident Response Management, 20%): use the incident-response lifecycle modules and tabletop-style scenario drills; practice ordering the six phases and naming the correct action for the phase you are in. Domain 4.0 (Reporting and Communication, 17%): use the security-writing and governance modules; practice rewriting one technical finding per day into a three-sentence executive summary. Worked example — a five-week plan. Weeks 1-2: domains 1 and 2 (the 63 percent core — log sets daily, prioritization drills daily). Week 3: domain 3 (incident scenarios, tabletop walkthroughs, evidence-handling drills). Week 4: domain 4 plus your weakest domain from practice scores (report-writing reps, metrics flashcards). Week 5: full timed sets only. The CySA+ practice gate: build 85-question-equivalent sets (or scaled 40-question halves), enforce the proportional time (roughly 2 minutes per question), and score by domain. Gate rule: overall 80 percent or better AND no domain below 70 percent. CySA+ punishes shallow breadth — because the questions are scenario-heavy, a domain at 60 percent means you cannot do the analyst work yet, not that you forgot a definition. Operator gotcha: on PBQs that present a log bundle, read the question stem FIRST, then hunt the logs for exactly what the stem asks. Analysts who read all the logs before reading the question burn ten minutes on data they never needed — the same discipline you will use in a real SOC queue. A second worked example - the weekly rhythm in practice. Monday's 20-minute log set surfaces a weakness: you keep misclassifying beaconing as normal traffic. Tuesday through Thursday, every triage set includes two beaconing scenarios until the pattern is reflex. Friday's assessment: a fresh 15-question set where beaconing appears three times - you nail all three, and the crosswalk table gets a 'validated' mark for the detection topic. That is the plan working as designed: the scorecard finds the weakness, the reps fix it, the assessment proves it. A common failure mode is studying without the Friday assessment - reps without measurement drift into comfortable repetition of what you already know. The assessment is the forcing function; never skip it. And a final gate nuance: when you pass twice consecutively, change nothing about your routine for the last week before the exam except tapering volume. Athletes taper; analysts should too - the goal on exam day is sharp recall, not exhausted cramming.

