The CISSP (Certified Information Systems Security Professional), administered by ISC2, is the current 2024 eight-domain version. Its published format: 100 to 150 computer-adaptive (CAT) questions, 3 hours, passing score 700 out of 1000. The eight domains and their 2024 weights: 1. Security and Risk Management, 16 percent; 2. Asset Security, 10 percent; 3. Security Architecture and Engineering, 13 percent; 4. Communication and Network Security, 13 percent; 5. Identity and Access Management, 13 percent; 6. Security Assessment and Testing, 12 percent; 7. Security Operations, 13 percent; 8. Software Development Security, 10 percent. Verify the sum: 16 + 10 + 13 + 13 + 13 + 12 + 13 + 10 = 100. Computer-adaptive testing means the exam adjusts to you: answer correctly and the next question gets harder; answer incorrectly and it gets easier, until the algorithm converges on your ability level. Worked example of what CAT feels like. You answer three governance questions correctly; the exam then serves a harder scenario: 'A multinational must comply with conflicting data-retention laws in two jurisdictions while supporting a fraud investigation — what is the best approach?' That question is testing whether your ability holds at a higher difficulty, not whether you memorized a definition. Practical consequence: you cannot count questions or predict the end — the exam stops when it has measured you (between 100 and 150 questions) or time expires. Pacing: 3 hours for up to 150 questions is about 72 seconds per question at the maximum length, but most candidates finish with fewer questions; the discipline is the same flag-and-return method, with one CAT-specific rule — you generally cannot go back to earlier questions, so each answer must be committed with the mindset rules, not revisited. Common mistake: treating CISSP like a harder Security+. It is a different species: Security+ tests what you know, CISSP tests how you decide. The candidate who knows every port but answers like a technician ('fix it myself, fast') loses to the candidate who answers like a manager ('assess the risk, follow the process, protect the business'). One more CAT mechanic to internalize: because the exam adapts, early questions matter disproportionately - they set the difficulty trajectory. A confident, careful first twenty questions earns you harder questions, and harder questions are worth more in the adaptive scoring. The practical rule: slow down at the start. Read every stem twice, eliminate the technician's answer first, then choose the manager's answer. Candidates who rush the opening and then settle in have already told the algorithm they are weaker than they are. Second, the 'cannot go back' rule changes guessing strategy: on a linear exam you skip and return, but on CAT each answer is final - so apply the mindset rules decisively, commit, and move on. Lingering on a CAT question does not buy you a better answer; it buys you time pressure on the questions the algorithm still needs to measure you.

