<text x="360.0" y="154" font-family="ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,SF Mono,Menlo,Consolas,monospace" font-size="11" fill="#8b949e" text-anchor="middle" font-weight="400" opacity="1.0" class="a-node" style="animation-delay:2.10s">mindset</text>
<text x="360.0" y="178" font-family="ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,SF Mono,Menlo,Consolas,monospace" font-size="11" fill="#8b949e" text-anchor="middle" font-weight="400" opacity="1.0" class="a-node" style="animation-delay:2.45s">shift</text></svg><figcaption style="margin-top:.6em;color:#8b949e;font-size:.85em;text-align:center"><strong>Fig. IL29-M35-D2 — </strong>The vocabulary this module installs — CISSP, format, testing…. If you can define each term in one plain sentence, the module&#x27;s objectives are within reach.</figcaption></figure>

Domains 1 and 2 are the governance foundation the other six assume. Domain 1, Security and Risk Management (16 percent — the heaviest domain), covers security governance principles, risk management concepts, policies and standards, legal and regulatory issues, and business continuity. This is where the exam tests whether you understand that security is a business function: risk assessments drive control selection, policies drive behavior, and laws drive notification and liability decisions. Domain 2, Asset Security (10 percent), covers information and asset classification, ownership roles, handling requirements, and data retention and destruction. Worked example — classification driving everything. A company classifies data as Public, Internal, Confidential, and Restricted. The customer database is classified Confidential. From that single decision flow the handling rules: encryption at rest and in transit, access limited to the finance and support roles, no storage on removable media, retention of seven years per the legal schedule, and destruction by cryptographic erasure. An exam scenario will give you the classification and ask for the handling — or give you the mishandling and ask what failed first (answer: the classification process, because protection without classification is guessing). Second worked example — ownership roles. The marketing director (data owner) decides who may access the campaign database and approves the access policy; the IT team (data custodian) implements the access controls and maintains the system; the users (data subjects in privacy contexts) are the people the data describes. The classic trap asks who 'owns' the server — the custodian maintains it, but the owner decides its data's fate. Common mistake: answering Domain 1 questions with Domain 7 tools. When the scenario is 'the board asks whether the security budget is adequate,' the answer involves risk assessment and metrics, not 'deploy EDR.' Governance questions want governance answers. A third worked example - the legal/regulatory discriminator. Scenario: 'A company operating in the EU suffers a breach of customer data.' The Domain 1 concepts in play: breach-notification duties under GDPR (the exam expects you to know that notification obligations exist and are time-bound, without demanding exact hour counts from memory), the need for legal counsel before public statements, and evidence preservation for potential investigation. The CISSP-correct sequence: contain, engage legal, preserve evidence, notify per the applicable regulation's process. The trap answers are 'notify customers immediately on social media' (legal reviews first) and 'delete the attacker artifacts to clean up' (destroys evidence). A second Domain 2 drill: data retention. 'The legal team requires financial records kept seven years; the privacy team wants minimal retention.' The resolution: the data owner balances the requirements in the retention policy - legal hold overrides deletion schedules, and the policy documents the decision. The exam loves these tension scenarios; the answer is always the documented policy decision, not either extreme.

