<text x="386.0" y="98" font-family="ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,SF Mono,Menlo,Consolas,monospace" font-size="12" fill="#f0f6fc" text-anchor="start" font-weight="400" opacity="1.0" class="a-node" style="animation-delay:2.80s">• Build a weighted study plan with a…</text></svg><figcaption style="margin-top:.6em;color:#8b949e;font-size:.85em;text-align:center"><strong>Fig. IL29-M35-D4 — </strong>Certification exams test two things: what you can explain (left) and what you can do (right). This module&#x27;s objectives split that way — drill the left column for recall, and practice the right column hands-on under time pressure.</figcaption></figure>

A CISSP study plan is a management exercise in itself: allocate by weight, drill the mindset, and measure with scenarios. Phase 1 — foundations (weeks 1-3): Domains 1 and 2 plus the mindset rules. Read governance and risk material until you can explain risk responses (avoid, transfer, mitigate, accept) and ownership roles without notes; write your personal list of the top 10 CISSP mindset rules. Phase 2 — technical core (weeks 4-7): Domains 3, 4, and 5. Build comparison tables: Bell-LaPadula vs. Biba vs. Clark-Wilson; MAC vs. DAC vs. RBAC; symmetric vs. asymmetric crypto concepts; network attacks vs. countermeasures. Phase 3 — verification and operations (weeks 8-9): Domains 6, 7, and 8. Drill assessment-type selection and SDLC security until the discriminators are instant. Phase 4 — integration (weeks 10-12): full scenario sets only, timed, with the decision log. Worked example — the decision log in action. Question: 'A new regulation requires breach notification within 72 hours. The current incident-response plan has no notification procedure. What should the CISO do first?' Tempting technical answer: 'Buy a breach-notification tool.' Correct management answer: 'Update the incident-response plan and assign notification responsibilities.' Decision-log entry: 'Governance before technology — the plan defines the duty; the tool only executes it.' Twenty such entries rewire your reflexes from engineer to manager. The CISSP practice gate: timed scenario sets of 40 questions in 75 minutes, scored by domain, with the gate at 75 percent overall and no domain below 60 percent. Because CAT adapts, your practice must include hard questions — if every practice question feels easy, you are not training at the difficulty the exam will actually serve you. Operator gotcha: experienced technicians fail CISSP by overthinking technical minutiae and underweighting the management answer. When you catch yourself debating cipher modes on a question whose stem is about policy, stop — the stem's first sentence tells you which domain (and which mindset) owns the question. A second worked example - the decision log catching a drift. Midway through Phase 4 you notice your log shows five entries where you picked the technical answer over the management one - all in Domain 7 scenarios about 'restoring service fast.' The pattern reveals your bias: under operations pressure you revert to technician. The fix is a personal rule added to the log's front page: 'In Domain 7, the plan is the answer - invoke the procedure before improvising.' Two weeks later the same scenario type appears and you pick the management answer without hesitation. That is the decision log working as a bias-correction instrument, not just a notebook. A final exam-week protocol: stop new material five days out; run only timed scenario sets and decision-log reviews. Sleep eight hours the last three nights - CAT punishes fatigue more than linear exams do, because every question demands judgment, and judgment is the first casualty of tiredness.

