<text x="136" y="432" font-family="ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,SF Mono,Menlo,Consolas,monospace" font-size="11" fill="#8b949e" text-anchor="start" font-weight="400" opacity="1.0" class="a-node" style="animation-delay:7.00s">Note 7 of 7</text></svg><figcaption style="margin-top:.6em;color:#8b949e;font-size:.85em;text-align:center"><strong>Fig. IL29-M36-D1 — </strong>The module&#x27;s full stack, top to bottom: start at The GSEC format: 106 questions,… and work down to Your GSEC study plan: knowledge…. Each layer builds on the one above it — skipping ahead leaves gaps.</figcaption></figure>

GSEC tests defense in depth as a design philosophy, not a slogan: layered, overlapping controls so that the failure of any single control does not equal compromise. The layers, outside in: physical security (locks, cameras, mantraps, environmental controls), perimeter and network security (firewalls, IDS/IPS, segmentation, VPNs), host security (hardening, patching, EDR, host firewalls), application security (secure configuration, input validation, patching), and data security (classification, encryption, DLP, backups). Worked example. A small office network: the perimeter firewall blocks unsolicited inbound traffic (network layer); the LAN is segmented so the guest Wi-Fi cannot reach the servers (network layer); workstations run EDR and are patched monthly (host layer); the line-of-business app enforces role-based access (application layer); the customer database is encrypted at rest and backed up immutably off-site (data layer). Now trace a failure: phishing delivers malware that the email filter misses (perimeter/app-layer miss) — EDR kills the payload on execution (host layer catches it). Or EDR misses a novel variant — but the workstation user is not a local admin, so the malware cannot persist (least privilege, host layer), and the firewall blocks its C2 beacon (network layer). Every incident becomes a story about which layer caught what the outer layers missed. Second worked example — the single-point-of-failure audit. List every control protecting the file server; for each, ask 'if this fails open tonight, what catches the attack?' Any asset with exactly one layer is a finding. Common mistakes: calling a firewall 'defense in depth' by itself (one layer is not depth); and forgetting the human layer — awareness training and tested incident response are layers too, and GSEC treats the people/process/technology triad as seriously as the network diagram. When a scenario asks for the best improvement, look for the answer that adds a layer where none exists rather than the answer that gilds a layer that is already strong. A third worked example - the layer-failure audit in action. 'The company relies on a single firewall for network security, with no host firewalls, no EDR, and flat internal networking.' The audit finding: four missing layers - host, detection, segmentation, and monitoring. The remediation priority: EDR on endpoints first (detects what the firewall misses), then host firewalls and segmentation (contains what EDR misses), then centralized logging (sees what everything missed). The exam's discriminator: the best next control is the one that covers the largest uncovered attack surface, not the one that strengthens an existing layer. A second nuance: defense in depth includes the human and process layers - the phishing-resistant culture from awareness training and the tested incident-response plan are layers with the same status as the firewall. When a scenario asks what 'completes' a defense-in-depth design, look for the missing people-or-process answer; the exam plants it as the correct choice among shiny technical options.

