<text x="360.0" y="178" font-family="ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,SF Mono,Menlo,Consolas,monospace" font-size="11" fill="#8b949e" text-anchor="middle" font-weight="400" opacity="1.0" class="a-node" style="animation-delay:2.45s">Defense</text></svg><figcaption style="margin-top:.6em;color:#8b949e;font-size:.85em;text-align:center"><strong>Fig. IL29-M36-D2 — </strong>The vocabulary this module installs — GSEC, format, questions…. If you can define each term in one plain sentence, the module&#x27;s objectives are within reach.</figcaption></figure>

Access control is the mechanism that decides who gets what, and GSEC expects fluency in its models and its daily administration. The four A's: identification (claiming an identity — a username), authentication (proving it — password, token, biometric), authorization (what the proven identity may do), and accountability (tracing actions to identities via logging). Authentication factors: something you know (password, PIN), something you have (token, smart card, phone), something you are (fingerprint, face). True multi-factor authentication combines factors from different categories — two passwords are still one factor. Authorization models: discretionary access control (DAC — the resource owner grants access, as in file-share permissions), mandatory access control (MAC — the system enforces policy based on labels, as in classified systems), and role-based access control (RBAC — access follows job function, the enterprise standard). Worked example — provisioning lifecycle. A new hire in finance: HR triggers provisioning (identification created), the account is issued with MFA enrollment (authentication), membership in the Finance role grants the shared-drive and ERP access (authorization via RBAC), and all access is logged to the SIEM (accountability). On termination day: the account is disabled within the defined window, the MFA tokens are revoked, and a quarterly access review would have caught any orphan. The exam's favorite failure in this lifecycle is the orphaned account — access that survives termination — and the correct fix is always process (automated deprovisioning tied to HR, plus periodic reviews), never a one-time cleanup. Second worked example — least privilege in practice. Users run as standard accounts; admin tasks require just-in-time elevation approved by policy. Malware executing in the user's context inherits standard-user limits and cannot install persistence — this is exactly the Elevation Control concept you know from ThreatLocker, now framed as the exam's principle. Common mistakes: confusing authentication with authorization (proving who you are vs. what you may do — the exam separates them in nearly every scenario); and choosing DAC answers where RBAC is correct at enterprise scale (DAC does not scale past a workgroup). A third worked example - the accountability drill. 'Three administrators share the root password on a Linux server; a destructive command runs at 3 AM and nobody admits it.' The failed A is accountability - shared credentials make actions untraceable. The fix has three parts: individual sudo accounts (identification + authentication per person), sudoers restricting who may run what (authorization), and centralized logging of every sudo invocation (accountability). The exam tests this as a before/after: the before violates accountability, the after restores it. Common trap: answering with 'stronger passwords.' The password was not the problem - the sharing was. When a stem shows shared accounts, the finding is always accountability, and the fix is always individual identities plus logging. A second trap: MFA on shared accounts. MFA strengthens authentication but does nothing for accountability - five people with the same MFA-enrolled account are still untraceable. Fix the identity model first.

