<text x="576" y="218" font-family="ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,SF Mono,Menlo,Consolas,monospace" font-size="11" fill="#3fb950" text-anchor="end" font-weight="600" opacity="1.0" class="a-node" style="animation-delay:7.70s">2</text></svg><figcaption style="margin-top:.6em;color:#8b949e;font-size:.85em;text-align:center"><strong>Fig. IL29-M36-D3 — </strong>What&#x27;s inside “GSEC Readiness”: 7 teaching notes and 6 objectives, backed by 28 practice components. Notes carry the teaching; everything else converts reading into recall.</figcaption></figure>

Two widely known entry-level paths are worth understanding as structure, not as magic: the Google Cybersecurity Professional Certificate, an entry-level professional certificate by Google delivered on Coursera, and the IBM Cybersecurity Analyst Professional Certificate, by IBM, also delivered on Coursera. Their public descriptions position them as structured, beginner-friendly sequences through cybersecurity foundations, tools, and analyst workflows — the kind of guided path that takes a learner from 'interested' to 'can do the basic analyst tasks.' This module's job is the crosswalk: mapping what those programs publicly describe covering to the IL-29 modules that go deeper, so you can use either certificate as a structured entry path alongside this course without duplicating effort. The framing, stated plainly and repeated in every certificate module: Independent preparation. Not affiliated with, endorsed by, or sponsored by Google, IBM, or Coursera; confers no certificate. IL-29 does not grant these certificates and is not connected to their issuers — it prepares you independently, and the certificates themselves are earned only through their own programs. Worked example — how to read a certificate's public page. Open the Google certificate page and list what it says it covers: treat each listed topic area as a row in your crosswalk table. For each row, ask two questions: (1) which IL-29 module covers this, and how deeply? (2) is there anything in the topic area that IL-29 does not cover? The rows with strong IL-29 coverage become 'validate via the certificate's own assessments' — you prove what you know without re-learning it. The rows with no IL-29 coverage become your gap list — sequenced study, each ending in a portfolio artifact. Common mistake: enrolling in a certificate and then studying everything twice — once in IL-29, once in the certificate — because no crosswalk was built. The crosswalk is a one-hour investment that saves weeks. A second worked example - the honest comparison. Google's certificate, as publicly described, is the broader on-ramp: foundations, analyst tools, and workflows for true beginners. IBM's is the analyst-role deepening: closer to SOC tasks and analytical thinking. Neither description claims to produce a senior operator - and that honesty is useful, because it tells you exactly where IL-29 takes over. The crosswalk's job is to draw that handoff line precisely: everything left of the line, validate via the certificate; everything right of it, build via IL-29 labs. A common mistake is choosing a certificate by brand instead of by fit. The fit question is simple: which of your gaps does its public topic map cover, in what sequence, and with what assessments? Answer that from the course pages - not from marketing - and the choice makes itself. And a standing caution for the whole module: certificate pages change. Re-verify the topic map from the public pages the week you build your crosswalk; a table built on last year's syllabus quietly misleads you about this year's gaps.

