<text x="386.0" y="150" font-family="ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,SF Mono,Menlo,Consolas,monospace" font-size="12" fill="#f0f6fc" text-anchor="start" font-weight="400" opacity="1.0" class="a-node" style="animation-delay:2.80s">• Convert every completed topic into a…</text></svg><figcaption style="margin-top:.6em;color:#8b949e;font-size:.85em;text-align:center"><strong>Fig. IL29-M37-D4 — </strong>Certification exams test two things: what you can explain (left) and what you can do (right). This module&#x27;s objectives split that way — drill the left column for recall, and practice the right column hands-on under time pressure.</figcaption></figure>

Everything you learn must convert into proof, because the Oct 13 panel — and every hiring process after it — evaluates demonstrated ability, not study history. The portfolio is the conversion mechanism: a public or shareable collection of labs, write-ups, and analyses that shows what you can do. For each certificate topic area you complete, produce exactly one artifact: a triage write-up (what the logs showed, what you concluded, what you did), a lab journal (what you built, what broke, how you fixed it), a report (executive summary or technical findings from a practice incident), or a configuration (a hardened baseline, a tuned detection rule, a firewall policy with rationale). Worked example — the artifact set for 'SIEM and log analysis.' Artifact 1: a triage write-up of a 50-event log set — 10 true positives identified, 3 false positives explained, mean triage time recorded. Artifact 2: a correlation rule you wrote, with its tuning history (v1 fired 200/day, v2 fires 4/day, zero false negatives on the test set). Artifact 3: a one-page executive summary of a practice incident derived from the same logs. Three artifacts, one topic area, undeniable proof. Second worked example — talking about it on the panel. 'I completed the SIEM content in both IL-29 and the IBM certificate' is a weak answer. 'I triaged a 50-event set, wrote a correlation rule that went from 200 false positives a day to 4 with no missed detections, and wrote the executive summary — here is the write-up' is a panel-winning answer. The certificate gave you the structure; the artifacts give you the story. Common mistake: keeping the portfolio private or unfinished. An artifact nobody can see might as well not exist — publish write-ups where the panel can find them (GitHub, a blog, LinkedIn articles), and finish every artifact before starting the next topic. The standing rule for this entire module: no topic is 'done' until its artifact exists. A second worked example - the artifact review ritual. Monthly, read your three oldest artifacts as if you were the panel. The triage write-up from month one: would it impress you now? If yes, your standards held. If it looks thin, that is growth - annotate what you would add today and let the annotation become a new drill. The portfolio is not a trophy case; it is a training log that happens to be public. A final caution about publishing: sanitize everything. Practice incidents use invented hostnames and IOCs - never real employer data, never real customer information. The panel respects operational security in your portfolio; a write-up leaking real internal details fails the interview it was meant to win. Build the habit now: every artifact gets a sanitization pass before it goes anywhere public. Proof of work, zero leakage - that is the professional standard.

