The foundational phase is where programs are won or lost: every defensive and offensive concept later in the program assumes you already speak the language — CIA triad, AAA, control types, crypto families, networking fundamentals, operating-system security basics. IL-29's prereq and early modules are the preparation ground, and the standard is not 'I have seen this' but 'I can teach this.' Worked example — the foundations self-test. Take each of these prompts and answer aloud without notes: explain the CIA triad with a real example of each leg breaking; explain symmetric vs. asymmetric crypto and why TLS uses both; explain TCP vs. UDP and name five ports with their services; explain least privilege and how it limits malware; explain the four A's of access control. Every prompt you cannot answer cleanly is a gap — and gaps in foundations compound: a shaky understanding of crypto makes the program's defensive crypto content twice as hard, because you are learning the foundation and the application simultaneously. Second worked example — closing a gap the IL-29 way. You stumble on 'explain how a firewall rule processes traffic.' The fix is not re-reading: it is the lab — write five firewall rules for a small office, test them mentally against six traffic scenarios, then explain the processing order aloud. Doing beats reading for foundations, because the program will assume you can do. Common mistake: skipping the self-test because the foundations 'feel familiar.' Familiarity is the enemy of readiness — the program moves at 10 to 20 hours a week, and every hour spent re-learning foundations is an hour not spent on the program's actual new material. The readiness checklist in this module's lab forces the honest score: foundations are either demonstrable or they are not, and 'sort of' counts as not. A third self-test prompt set, because foundations have more corners than learners expect. Explain DNS resolution from keystroke to IP address. Explain what happens when you type a URL and press enter - the full chain: DNS, TCP handshake, TLS negotiation, HTTP request. Explain the difference between a hub, a switch, and a router in one sentence each. Explain symmetric vs. asymmetric crypto with the TLS handshake as the example. Four prompts, each answerable in 90 seconds by someone whose foundations are solid - and each exposing a specific gap when they are not. The 'type a URL' question is the classic: it touches DNS, TCP, TLS, HTTP, and rendering, and a fluent answer proves integrated understanding rather than isolated facts. If any prompt stalls you, the remediation is the same IL-29 loop: the relevant module, the lab, the teach-back. A final foundations trap: confusing familiarity with fluency. You have SEEN the TCP handshake a dozen times; can you DRAW it from memory, with sequence numbers, right now? Fluency is the standard - the program assumes it on day one.

