The defensive phase — protecting systems, detecting attacks, responding to incidents — is IL-29's home turf, and it is where your preparation should be deepest. The program's defensive arc assumes you can think like a defender: read logs, tune detections, prioritize vulnerabilities, run incidents, harden systems. IL-29's SIEM, log-analysis, detection-engineering, vulnerability-management, incident-response, and Windows/Linux-hardening modules map directly here, and the preparation standard is operator-grade: labs completed, artifacts produced, timed reps passed. Worked example — the defensive readiness drill. Build a practice incident: a phishing email delivers a payload, EDR fires, and you must triage (is it a true positive?), scope (how far did it spread?), contain (isolate, block C2, disable accounts), eradicate (remove persistence, close the vector), recover (reimage, restore, monitor), and report (executive summary plus technical timeline). Run it end to end, timed, with a written record. If you can do that cleanly, the program's defensive phase will feel like structured review rather than new terrain — which is exactly the position you want, because new terrain at 10-20 hours a week is exhausting. Second worked example — mapping NIST frameworks to defense. The program covers NIST frameworks; your preparation is a one-page reference per framework purpose: what it organizes, what questions it answers, where it fits in a security program. You are not memorizing clause numbers — you are building the mental index that lets the program's framework content land on prepared ground. MITRE ATT&CK gets the same treatment: know it as the adversary-tactics knowledge base — tactics, techniques, and procedures organized so defenders can map what they see to what the attacker is doing — and practice the mapping with IL-29's ATT&CK modules. Common mistake: preparing for the defensive phase with reading instead of reps. Defense is a doing discipline; the program will test doing. Arrive already doing. A third defensive drill - the detection-engineering rep. Write a detection rule for credential dumping: what log source (Windows Security log, specific event IDs), what pattern (LSASS access from an unusual process), what threshold (any single occurrence - this is never benign), what response (isolate, alert as critical). Then tune it: the backup agent touches LSASS legitimately every night - add the exclusion with a comment explaining why, and document the residual risk. That write-tune-document cycle IS defensive operations at the practitioner level, and doing it three times for three techniques builds more readiness than reading about detection for a week. A second defensive nuance: the program's defensive arc will assume you understand the kill chain as the attacker's complement to your IR lifecycle. Practice the pairing: for each IR phase, name the attacker phase it counters - detection counters execution, containment counters lateral movement, eradication counters persistence. The pairing turns two memorized lists into one understood conflict.

