The readiness checklist is the go/no-go instrument for the $7,750 decision. Score each item honestly — ready, close, or not yet — and do not apply until every item is ready. Item 1: foundations demonstrable — you pass the foundations self-test from note 2 without notes. Item 2: defensive reps done — you have completed the IL-29 defensive labs and can run the practice incident from note 3 end to end. Item 3: offensive literacy — you can replay an attack chain as the attacker and map it to ATT&CK tactics. Item 4: framework familiarity — you can state the purpose of the NIST frameworks, ATT&CK, and the CSA CCM in your own words. Item 5: time — 10 to 20 hours per week are actually free in your calendar for 24 consecutive weeks (plan for the high end; life does not pause for programs). Item 6: finances — the $7,750 is budgeted as a planned investment with no impact on essentials. Item 7: capstone draft — a written proposal exists, grounded in your IL-29 artifacts. Item 8: application materials — whatever the program requires is prepared, not improvised. Worked example — scoring honestly. You score: foundations ready, defensive reps close (two labs left), offensive literacy not yet, frameworks ready, time close (14 hrs/week free but fragile), finances ready, capstone draft not yet, application materials not yet. The verdict: not yet — with a dated plan: finish the two labs in two weeks, complete the attacker-replay exercise in week three, draft the capstone in week four, re-score. That is a one-month path from 'not yet' to 'ready,' and it is infinitely cheaper than enrolling unready and drowning. Second worked example — the time audit. List your actual week hour by hour; highlight the 10-20 hours the program needs. If the hours come from sleep, the plan fails — programs do not survive on borrowed sleep. If they come from low-value time you can genuinely reallocate, the plan holds. Common mistake: treating the checklist as pessimism. It is the opposite — it is how you protect a $7,750 investment and 24 weeks of your life. Enroll ready, and the program is a credential; enroll unready, and it is an expensive lesson in overcommitment. A final checklist discipline: the pre-application review. One week before applying, do a full mock week at the program's pace - 15 hours of structured study across the topic map, timed and logged. If the week feels sustainable, your time audit was honest. If it breaks you, the audit lied - and better to learn that from a mock week than from week three of a $7,750 program. The mock week is the last and most honest readiness instrument in this module: it tests the plan against reality before reality starts charging tuition.

