Zero trust is the philosophy behind every ThreatLocker module, and the panel will expect you to speak it fluently — not as a slogan, but as operational reasoning. The principles: never trust, always verify (no implicit trust based on network location — inside the LAN is not safe); assume breach (design as if the attacker is already inside); verify explicitly (authenticate and authorize every access decision with all available signals); least privilege (every identity and process gets the minimum access it needs, nothing more); and microsegmentation (divide the environment so a compromise cannot move laterally). Worked example — the panel classic. 'Why is zero trust better than a strong perimeter?' Your 60-second answer: 'A perimeter assumes everything inside is trustworthy, so one breached workstation means the whole LAN is exposed — the attacker moves laterally at will. Zero trust removes that assumption: every application launch is verified against policy (allowlisting), every application's capabilities are constrained (ringfencing), no user holds standing admin (elevation control), storage and network access are granted per-policy (storage and network control). The 2026 reality is that phishing beats perimeters weekly; zero trust assumes the phish succeeded and still contains the damage.' Second worked example — mapping principles to modules for the panel. Never-trust-always-verify becomes allowlisting (verify every executable). Least privilege becomes elevation control (no standing admin) plus ringfencing (apps get minimum capability). Assume breach becomes network control (dynamic ACLs limit lateral movement) plus Detect (alert on what the modules block). Microsegmentation becomes network control policies per endpoint. When the panel asks 'what does zero trust mean to you,' answer with the principles AND the module mapping — concept plus operation is what separates a candidate who read the website from one who can do the job. Common mistake: describing zero trust as a product you buy. It is an architecture and a discipline; ThreatLocker's modules are enforcement mechanisms for it. The panel wants operators who understand the philosophy deeply enough to make policy decisions, not recite marketing. TECHNICAL BANK 1 — zero trust: Q1: 'Define zero trust in one sentence.' A: 'A security model that eliminates implicit trust and verifies every access decision explicitly, assuming breach.' Q2: 'Why does network location no longer imply trust?' A: 'Phishing, VPN compromises, and insider threats routinely place attackers inside the perimeter; location is not identity.' Q3: 'How does least privilege limit ransomware?' A: 'Ransomware inherits the privileges of its host process; standard-user execution plus no standing admin blocks persistence and lateral movement.' Q4: 'What is microsegmentation's goal?' A: 'Contain breaches by dividing the environment so compromise of one segment cannot reach others.' Q5: 'Name the five principles.' A: 'Never trust always verify, assume breach, verify explicitly, least privilege, microsegmentation.'

