<text x="636.0" y="104" font-family="ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,SF Mono,Menlo,Consolas,monospace" font-size="11" fill="#8b949e" text-anchor="middle" font-weight="400" opacity="1.0" class="a-node" style="animation-delay:3.85s">Note 4</text></svg><figcaption style="margin-top:.6em;color:#8b949e;font-size:.85em;text-align:center"><strong>Fig. IL29-M39-D1 — </strong>Read the notes in order — Zero-trust concepts: the… lays the foundation and Elevation Control: least… brings it together. Each note assumes the vocabulary of the ones before it.</figcaption></figure>

Allowlisting (Application Control) is ThreatLocker's deny-by-default core: it learns the application environment, permits what users use, and denies everything else. Understand the inversion deeply, because the panel will probe it: traditional antivirus is default-allow with blocklists — everything runs unless it matches a known-bad signature, so novel malware walks through. Allowlisting is default-deny — nothing runs unless it is known-good, so novel malware is stopped without anyone needing to recognize it. The learning phase matters operationally: on deployment, the platform observes the environment to learn which applications users actually run, and policy is built from that observed reality — then enforcement denies everything outside it. Worked example — the panel scenario. 'A customer asks why their new accounting tool was blocked.' Your answer: 'Allowlisting denied it because it was not in the learned permitted set — that is the system working as designed. The workflow is: verify the tool is legitimate and approved, add it to policy through the proper change process, and it runs. The block is not an error; it is the default-deny guarantee. If we allowed unknown executables while we checked, we would not have allowlisting.' Second worked example — the zero-day question. 'How does allowlisting stop malware nobody has seen before?' Answer: 'It does not need to see it. The malware is an unknown executable, and unknown executables do not run — full stop. Signature-based tools must recognize the threat; allowlisting only needs to recognize the permitted set, which is finite and known.' Common mistake: confusing allowlisting with whitelisting-as-a-list you maintain by hand. The operational reality is learning plus policy: the platform learns the environment, policy codifies the decisions, and changes flow through approval — not through a spreadsheet someone edits. Another trap: promising the panel that allowlisting 'stops all malware.' The honest operator answer: it stops unauthorized execution, which kills the vast majority of malware delivery — and ringfencing, elevation control, and network control cover what execution alone cannot. TECHNICAL BANK 2 — allowlisting: Q6: 'Default-allow vs default-deny in one sentence each.' A: 'Default-allow runs everything except known-bad; default-deny runs only known-good.' Q7: 'What happens during the learning phase?' A: 'The platform observes which applications the environment actually uses, so policy reflects operational reality.' Q8: 'A legitimate new app is blocked. Is this a failure?' A: 'No — it is default-deny working. Verify, approve through change control, add to policy.' Q9: 'Why does allowlisting not need malware signatures?' A: 'It evaluates the executable against the permitted set, not against a threat database.' Q10: 'What is the operator's job in an allowlisting deployment?' A: 'Learn the environment, build policy from observed use, handle approval requests, and audit the permitted set.'

