<text x="360.0" y="154" font-family="ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,SF Mono,Menlo,Consolas,monospace" font-size="11" fill="#8b949e" text-anchor="middle" font-weight="400" opacity="1.0" class="a-node" style="animation-delay:2.10s">expects</text>
<text x="360.0" y="178" font-family="ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,SF Mono,Menlo,Consolas,monospace" font-size="11" fill="#8b949e" text-anchor="middle" font-weight="400" opacity="1.0" class="a-node" style="animation-delay:2.45s">speak</text></svg><figcaption style="margin-top:.6em;color:#8b949e;font-size:.85em;text-align:center"><strong>Fig. IL29-M39-D2 — </strong>The vocabulary this module installs — Zero-trust, concepts, philosophy…. If you can define each term in one plain sentence, the module&#x27;s objectives are within reach.</figcaption></figure>

Ringfencing is the module the panel will press hardest on, because it is ThreatLocker's distinctive answer to the hardest problem in endpoint security: the trusted application turned weapon. Ringfencing restricts what allowed applications can do — it can block them from executing other applications or processes, editing the registry, reading or writing files, and opening network sessions. The threat model: the application itself is legitimate and permitted (Word, a browser, a line-of-business app), but an attacker exploits it — a malicious document, a drive-by, a macro — and uses the trusted app as the launchpad. Without ringfencing, the trusted app's privileges become the attacker's privileges. With ringfencing, the app runs but its capabilities are caged. Worked example — the canonical scenario. A user opens a Word document with a malicious macro. Word is allowed; the macro tries to launch PowerShell to download a payload. Ringfencing blocks Word from executing other processes — the attack dies at the launch attempt, and Detect logs the blocked indicator. Your panel answer: 'The document opened, the user saw no disruption, the weaponization was stopped, and we have the forensic record.' Second worked example — the business-app dilemma. A customer's legacy application is required for operations but has a known history of exploitation. Uninstalling is not an option; allowing it unrestricted is not acceptable. The operator's answer: permit the application via allowlisting (it is known-good and needed), then ringfence it aggressively — no child processes, no registry writes, no unexpected network sessions, file access limited to its working directories. The business keeps its app; the attacker loses every weaponization path. That tradeoff reasoning — security without breaking operations — is exactly what the IT Infrastructure Specialist role does daily. Common mistake: describing ringfencing as 'blocking bad apps.' It constrains good apps. The exam-style discriminator: allowlisting decides WHAT runs; ringfencing decides what running things MAY DO. Memorize that sentence — it answers half the module-confusion questions. TECHNICAL BANK 3 — ringfencing: Q11: 'What does ringfencing restrict?' A: 'The capabilities of allowed applications: executing other processes, registry edits, file read/write, network sessions.' Q12: 'Word launches PowerShell via macro. What happens?' A: 'Ringfencing blocks the child-process execution; Detect logs the blocked indicator.' Q13: 'Allowlisting vs ringfencing in one line.' A: 'Allowlisting decides what runs; ringfencing decides what running things may do.' Q14: 'A required legacy app has known exploits. Your recommendation?' A: 'Allowlist it as known-good and needed, then ringfence its capabilities tightly.' Q15: 'Does ringfencing break the application?' A: 'Properly scoped, no — it constrains capabilities the app does not legitimately need.'

