<text x="386.0" y="150" font-family="ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,SF Mono,Menlo,Consolas,monospace" font-size="12" fill="#f0f6fc" text-anchor="start" font-weight="400" opacity="1.0" class="a-node" style="animation-delay:3.15s">• Answer 40+ technical questions aloud…</text></svg><figcaption style="margin-top:.6em;color:#8b949e;font-size:.85em;text-align:center"><strong>Fig. IL29-M39-D4 — </strong>Certification exams test two things: what you can explain (left) and what you can do (right). This module&#x27;s objectives split that way — drill the left column for recall, and practice the right column hands-on under time pressure.</figcaption></figure>

The panel will also test generalist infrastructure skill — Windows endpoint troubleshooting and networking fundamentals — because the IT Infrastructure Specialist supports the whole endpoint, not just the ThreatLocker agent. Windows troubleshooting order: check Event Viewer (System and Application logs) for the error and its timestamp, check the application's own logs, verify recent changes (updates, GPO, agent installs), reproduce the issue, and escalate with the evidence bundle. Networking fundamentals: the bottom-up order — physical (cable, link lights, Wi-Fi association), IP configuration (ipconfig: address, mask, gateway, DNS), gateway reachability (ping the gateway), DNS resolution (nslookup), then the service itself (port check, application log). Memorize the discriminators: 'can ping 8.8.8.8 but cannot browse' is DNS; '169.254.x.x address' is DHCP failure (APIPA); 'works on Wi-Fi but not Ethernet' is the wired path (cable, switch port, NIC). Worked example — the post-agent-install ticket. 'After the ThreatLocker agent was installed, the accounting app cannot sync.' Your troubleshooting: confirm the app worked before (isolate the change), check Detect for blocked indicators from that app (is a module denying it?), check Ringfencing policy on the app (blocked child process or network session?), check Network Control (needed port closed?), then resolve via policy — not by disabling the agent. The panel loves this scenario because the wrong answer ('uninstall the agent') trades security for convenience, and the right answer fixes it with policy. Second worked example — the interview-day scoring rubric you will hold yourself to. Per question, 6 points: accuracy (0-2: correct facts, correct module, correct reasoning), structure (0-2: what, why, operational implication — in that order), concision (0-1: 60-90 seconds, then stop), honesty (0-1: 'I don't know, but here is how I would find out' beats an invented fact — inventing facts is an automatic zero for the question). Forty questions at 6 points is 240; the gate is 85 percent (204). The honesty rule is the most important: panels forgive 'I don't know,' they never forgive bluffing. TECHNICAL BANK 8 — troubleshooting and fundamentals: Q36: 'Workstation cannot reach the file server. Order?' A: 'Physical, ipconfig, ping gateway, nslookup/DNS, then the service.' Q37: 'Can ping 8.8.8.8, cannot browse. Diagnosis?' A: 'DNS failure — nslookup to confirm, then check DNS server config.' Q38: '169.254.x.x address. What failed?' A: 'DHCP — no lease; check DHCP server, relay, and link.' Q39: 'App crashes on one workstation only. First look?' A: 'Event Viewer Application log, then the app's own logs.' Q40: 'App breaks after agent install. The wrong fix and the right fix?' A: 'Wrong: disable the agent. Right: check Detect and module policy, resolve via policy.'

