STAR is the structure every behavioral answer needs: Situation (the specific incident — one event, not your career), Task (what you were responsible for — the mission you owned), Action (what YOU did, step by step, in first person), Result (the measurable outcome — numbers, time, money, satisfaction). The panel asks behavioral questions because past behavior predicts future performance, and STAR is how you make your past legible in 60 to 90 seconds. Worked example — a scaffold with placeholders, NOT a real story. S: '[A real 2 AM alert you triaged — name the system and the symptom.]' T: '[What you owned that night — triage, containment, communication.]' A: '[Your exact steps in order: verified the alert, scoped the fleet, isolated the hosts, documented the timeline.]' R: '[Measured outcome: contained in X minutes, Y hosts affected, playbook updated with Z change.]' Notice the discipline: every bracket is a prompt for YOUR real history. The scaffold teaches the shape; only your life fills it. Nothing in this module invents your history — any example that looks like a story is a labeled scaffold with empty brackets, and your lab work is filling those brackets truthfully. Second worked example — the timing math. Ninety seconds at conversational pace is roughly 180-220 words: about 40 words for Situation, 30 for Task, 90 for Action, 40 for Result. The Action gets the most words because the panel is hiring your judgment under pressure — what you did, in what order, and why. Practice with a stopwatch until the shape is muscle memory: when nerves hit on Oct 13, the four beats give you rails. Common mistake: spending 60 seconds on the Situation. The panel does not need the company's history — it needs the incident, your mission, your moves, and the score. A second classic: 'we' instead of 'I.' Team context gets 'we'; every action beat gets 'I.' If your Action paragraph has zero first-person verbs, rewrite it. A third worked example - the 90-second budget as a rehearsal tool. Record yourself answering one scaffold aloud, then transcribe it and count the words per beat. Typical first attempts: 100 words of Situation, 20 of Task, 60 of Action, 10 of Result - the exact inversion of the ideal. The transcription does not lie, and one round of it fixes the shape permanently: cut the Situation to the incident, expand the Action to your steps, and give the Result its numbers. A second timing trick: the headline. Open every answer with a one-sentence headline ('I contained a three-host incident in 20 minutes') before the STAR detail. The headline buys you the panel's attention for the remaining 75 seconds - and if nerves ever cut an answer short, the headline alone still landed the point. Practice headlines separately: one sentence per story, specific and quantified, until they are automatic.

