<text x="360.0" y="178" font-family="ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,SF Mono,Menlo,Consolas,monospace" font-size="11" fill="#8b949e" text-anchor="middle" font-weight="400" opacity="1.0" class="a-node" style="animation-delay:2.45s">shape</text></svg><figcaption style="margin-top:.6em;color:#8b949e;font-size:.85em;text-align:center"><strong>Fig. IL29-M40-D2 — </strong>The vocabulary this module installs — STAR, refresher, four…. If you can define each term in one plain sentence, the module&#x27;s objectives are within reach.</figcaption></figure>

The support-flavored prompts test the Cyber Hero dimension of the role: customers, frustration, and service under pressure. Prompt 1: 'Tell me about a difficult customer or frustrated user you supported.' The panel wants de-escalation plus resolution — listen first, acknowledge the frustration without defensiveness, diagnose systematically, resolve, and follow up. Prompt 2: 'How do you handle a customer who is upset about a blocked application?' This is the ThreatLocker-specific version: the allowlisting block from M39 becomes a service interaction — explain the protection plainly, verify legitimacy, shepherd the approval, follow up when it is permitted. Prompt 3: 'Describe a time you had to say no to a request.' The panel tests backbone with empathy — the secure answer delivered kindly, with the alternative offered. For each prompt, build two scaffolds in your lab: S-[the real interaction], T-[what you owned], A-[your exact de-escalation and resolution steps], R-[measured outcome: resolution time, satisfaction, repeat-contact avoided]. Worked example — scaffolding Prompt 2 without inventing. S: '[A real time a security control blocked legitimate work.]' T: '[Your responsibility: resolve the block without weakening security.]' A: '[How you explained the block in plain language, verified the software, processed the approval, confirmed the fix with the user.]' R: '[Time to resolution, the user's response, any process improvement you suggested.]' The scaffold is complete and honest — and completely empty of invented facts. Your job in the lab is to fill it with one real memory. Common mistake: answering support prompts with pure technical detail. The panel is scoring the human interaction — lead with how you handled the person, then the technical resolution. A second trap: badmouthing the customer, even lightly. 'The user was being unreasonable' fails the prompt; 'the user was frustrated because their deadline was at risk, so I...' passes it. A fourth support prompt for the bank - Prompt 3b: 'How do you explain a technical problem to a non-technical person?' The Cyber Hero answer: analogies anchored in the user's world, no jargon, check for understanding, and the fix framed as what THEY need to do (one clear action). Scaffold: S-[a real explanation you gave], T-[what the user needed to understand or do], A-[your explanation approach: the analogy, the single action item, the confirmation], R-[the user succeeded / the ticket closed cleanly]. This prompt appears constantly in support-adjacent roles because it tests the rarest combination: deep technical understanding expressed simply. The trap is the expert's curse - explaining at YOUR level instead of theirs. The fix is the teach-back: 'just to make sure I explained it clearly, could you tell me what you will do next?' - which verifies understanding while respecting the user. Practice one teach-back line until it sounds natural, not scripted.

