Teamwork prompts test collaboration: communication, handoffs, and conflict handled like an adult. Prompt 12: 'Tell me about a time you worked effectively on a team.' The panel wants YOUR collaboration mechanics — how you communicated, how handoffs worked, what you owned inside the team effort. Prompt 13: 'Describe a disagreement with a coworker and how you resolved it.' No villains: the disagreement stated fairly, your approach (listen, data, compromise or escalate properly), the resolution. Prompt 14: 'Tell me about a time you helped a colleague.' Generosity with a real example — what you taught or unblocked, and the outcome. Prompt 15: 'Why ThreatLocker, and why this role?' The closing prompt — connect your trajectory to their mission honestly: zero-trust operations, the Cyber Hero service dimension, and what you bring on day one. Scaffold teamwork stories with the collaboration beats: S-[the real team situation], T-[your specific part], A-[your communication, handoffs, and any friction you navigated], R-[the team outcome plus your measurable contribution]. Worked example — scaffolding Prompt 13. S: '[A real professional disagreement.]' T: '[What was at stake and your role.]' A: '[How you engaged: listened first, brought data, proposed options, accepted the decision or escalated properly.]' R: '[The resolution and the working relationship afterward.]' The R-beat must include the relationship afterward — panels know that unresolved conflict poisons teams, and they are checking that you repair. Common mistake: the conflict story where the other person was simply wrong and you heroically corrected them. That is not conflict resolution; that is a victory lap, and it reads as someone difficult to work with. Show the legitimate other perspective, then your path through it. For Prompt 15, prepare the honest version now: connect your real path — IT operations, the security depth built in this course, the service orientation — to what the role actually does. Specific beats generic: 'your allowlisting-plus-ringfencing model is the most operator-honest endpoint approach I have studied, and the Cyber Hero side matches how I work' beats 'I love cybersecurity.' A final logistics layer: the 24 hours before. Confirm the route and parking the night before - a lost candidate arrives flustered, and flustered fails STAR. Prepare your bag: ID, printed resume copies, a notebook, water. Eat a real breakfast; low blood sugar destroys 90-second discipline. And set the mental frame on the drive: you are not begging for a job, you are showing a panel how you work - the rehearsal already proved you can do it. That frame shift from supplicant to demonstrator changes posture, voice, and answers. Walk in as the operator you have spent weeks becoming, not the applicant hoping to become one. The work is done. October 13 is the demonstration.

