<text x="360.0" y="218" font-family="ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,SF Mono,Menlo,Consolas,monospace" font-size="11" fill="#8b949e" text-anchor="middle" font-weight="400" opacity="1.0" class="a-node" style="animation-delay:7.35s">visual guides</text>
<text x="576" y="218" font-family="ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,SF Mono,Menlo,Consolas,monospace" font-size="11" fill="#3fb950" text-anchor="end" font-weight="600" opacity="1.0" class="a-node" style="animation-delay:7.70s">2</text></svg><figcaption style="margin-top:.6em;color:#8b949e;font-size:.85em;text-align:center"><strong>Fig. IL29-M40-D3 — </strong>What&#x27;s inside “STAR Behavioral Bank for the Panel”: 7 teaching notes and 6 objectives, backed by 28 practice components. Notes carry the teaching; everything else converts reading into recall.</figcaption></figure>

NIST Special Publication 800-61 organizes incident response into four phases that loop, not a straight line: (1) Preparation — everything built before an incident, from team roles to tested backups; (2) Detection and Analysis — noticing something wrong, confirming it is real, and scoping how bad it is; (3) Containment, Eradication, and Recovery — stopping the damage, removing the cause, and bringing systems back safely; (4) Post-Incident Activity — learning from what happened so it does not repeat. The phases feed back into each other: analysis during containment often sends you back to re-scope, and post-incident findings rewrite your preparation. Worked example: at 2:00 AM a monitoring alert fires — ransomware encrypting files on three workstations. Detection and analysis (2:00-2:40): you confirm the alert is real, find the initial phishing email, and discover 11 more hosts with the same malware beaconing. Containment (2:40-4:00): you isolate all 14 hosts from the network but leave them powered on to preserve memory. Eradication (day 2): you identify the malicious scheduled task and rogue account, remove them fleet-wide, and patch the exploited vulnerability. Recovery (days 3-5): you restore the file server from a tested backup and bring departments back in priority order with extra monitoring. Post-incident (week 2): the review finds the phishing email bypassed the filter — action item: tune the filter and run a targeted phishing drill. Common mistakes: treating the phases as a checklist you visit once (real incidents bounce between detection and containment for hours); skipping preparation and improvising under pressure; and jumping to eradication — wiping machines — before scoping, which destroys evidence and leaves the entry point open for reinfection. A detail worth internalizing: the phases are drawn as a cycle because real incidents refuse to stay in one box. You contain three hosts, then firewall logs reveal a fourth beaconing host you missed — you are back in detection and analysis, re-scoping, before containment resumes. The current revision of the guidance is NIST SP 800-61 Revision 2, and its core message has not changed across revisions: coordination and preparation matter more than tools. Another worked micro-example: during eradication of the ransomware case, the team discovers the phishing email also reached the billing department's shared mailbox, which auto-forwards to an external accountant. That single finding re-opens scoping (is the accountant's machine compromised?), extends containment (block the forwarding rule), and adds a post-incident action item (review all mailbox forwarding rules quarterly). One artifact, three phases touched. Operator gotcha for the exam-minded: when a question asks 'which phase,' look for the verb — building, testing, and documenting are preparation; confirming and scoping are detection and analysis; isolating and blocking are containment; removing root cause is eradication; restoring service is recovery; reviewing and reporting are post-incident. The verbs decide.

