<text x="636.0" y="84" font-family="ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,SF Mono,Menlo,Consolas,monospace" font-size="14" fill="#f0f6fc" text-anchor="middle" font-weight="700" opacity="1.0" class="a-node" style="animation-delay:3.50s">Containment:…</text>
<text x="636.0" y="104" font-family="ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,SF Mono,Menlo,Consolas,monospace" font-size="11" fill="#8b949e" text-anchor="middle" font-weight="400" opacity="1.0" class="a-node" style="animation-delay:3.85s">Note 4</text></svg><figcaption style="margin-top:.6em;color:#8b949e;font-size:.85em;text-align:center"><strong>Fig. IL29-M41-D1 — </strong>Read the notes in order — The four phases: NIST SP… lays the foundation and Containment: stop the bleeding… brings it together. Each note assumes the vocabulary of the ones before it.</figcaption></figure>

Preparation is the phase you control completely, and it decides how the other three go. A prepared organization has: named IR roles with backups (who is incident commander at 2 AM, and who is their backup); an incident response playbook with call trees and decision authority (who can authorize taking the file server offline); out-of-band communication — a way to talk that does not depend on the systems under attack (a separate chat channel, phone tree, or even a conference bridge number printed on paper); tooling ready to go (EDR console access, forensic imaging tools, known-good OS install media, clean backup verification); and relationships established in advance (legal counsel, HR for insider cases, law enforcement contacts, your cyber-insurance carrier's breach hotline). Worked example: a one-page prep checklist for a 150-endpoint clinic. Roles: incident commander (IT manager), technical lead (you), communications lead (office manager), scribe (whoever is free — they log every decision with timestamps). Contacts: MSP after-hours number, backup vendor support, cyber-insurance breach line, local FBI field office. Tooling: EDR admin credentials in a sealed envelope, two USB drives with clean Windows install media, a 2 TB external drive for forensic images, the phone-tree printed and taped inside the server room. Cadence: tabletop exercise quarterly, backup restore test monthly, contact list verified every six months. Operator gotchas: the playbook that lives only in someone's head is not a playbook; credentials for your IR tools must not be stored solely in the password manager that lives on the compromised network — keep an offline copy; and never assume the backup works — an untested backup is a rumor, so restore-test on schedule and record the result. Two preparation artifacts separate mature teams from improvisers: the jump bag and the tested restore. The jump bag — physical or virtual — holds everything the technical lead needs in the first hour: a laptop with forensic tools, write-blocker, external drives, clean OS media, console cables, and the printed phone tree. Build yours before you need it; during the incident is the wrong time to discover your USB drive is corrupted. On backups, preparation means proving the restore, not assuming it: schedule monthly restore tests of a sample system and a sample file share, record the result (success, duration, data integrity check), and store the log where the incident team can find it at 2 AM. A backup that has never been restored is a hope, not a control. Also prepare your evidence handling in advance: designate where forensic images go (an encrypted external drive in the safe, with a custody log taped to it), decide your hashing standard (SHA-256), and confirm you have legal's guidance on what to preserve if the incident might involve employee misconduct. Final gotcha: preparation decays. People change roles, phone numbers change, tools get replaced. Put a six-month expiry on the contact list and the playbook review, with a named owner — otherwise your beautiful plan is three years out of date when the alert fires.

