<text x="360.0" y="154" font-family="ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,SF Mono,Menlo,Consolas,monospace" font-size="11" fill="#8b949e" text-anchor="middle" font-weight="400" opacity="1.0" class="a-node" style="animation-delay:2.10s">response</text>
<text x="360.0" y="178" font-family="ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,SF Mono,Menlo,Consolas,monospace" font-size="11" fill="#8b949e" text-anchor="middle" font-weight="400" opacity="1.0" class="a-node" style="animation-delay:2.45s">lifecycle</text></svg><figcaption style="margin-top:.6em;color:#8b949e;font-size:.85em;text-align:center"><strong>Fig. IL29-M41-D2 — </strong>The vocabulary this module installs — four, phases, NIST…. If you can define each term in one plain sentence, the module&#x27;s objectives are within reach.</figcaption></figure>

NIST 800-61 distinguishes precursors — signs an incident may occur in the future (a vulnerability scan against your firewall, a threat intel report about ransomware targeting healthcare) — from indicators — signs an incident is occurring or has occurred (an EDR alert for credential dumping, a user report of encrypted files, outbound traffic to a known command-and-control server). Detection sources include your own alerts, users calling the help desk, and third parties (an ISP or a partner telling you they see malicious traffic from your network). Analysis then answers three questions: is it real (triage out false positives), what is the scope (one host or fifty), and what is the priority. NIST suggests prioritizing by functional impact (how much of the business is down), information impact (was sensitive data accessed or stolen), and recoverability (can we rebuild from clean backups, or is this existential). Worked example: 9:00 AM, your ThreatLocker Detect-style console shows one alert — 'powershell.exe launched by winword.exe' on a single workstation, blocked. Triage: real behavior, but blocked before damage; scope is one host; priority moderate — you investigate the email that delivered the document. Contrast: 2:00 AM, fourteen hosts beaconing to an external IP and file shares encrypting. Scope is fleet-wide, functional impact is severe, information impact unknown — that is a full incident, wake everyone up. Common mistakes: treating every alert as a five-alarm fire (alert fatigue burns out your team and teaches everyone to ignore the console); the opposite — assuming a single blocked alert needs no follow-up (the block stopped this attempt, but the phishing email that delivered it may have reached fifty inboxes); and scoping by gut feel instead of evidence — always check: same email to other users, same hash on other hosts, same outbound connections in firewall logs. Dwell time — the gap between initial compromise and detection — is the statistic that should haunt you. Industry reports have historically measured dwell in weeks or months for incidents discovered by third parties; your goal is to drive yours toward hours through better detection and user reporting. Practically, scoping is where analysis lives or dies. Beyond checking which users opened the attachment, pull firewall and DNS logs for the indicators: which internal hosts resolved the malicious domain, which hosts made the outbound connection, and when did each start. One clinic case: the EDR showed three encrypted hosts, but DNS logs showed eleven hosts resolving the attacker's domain over the prior week — the true scope was eleven, and the extra eight were caught before encryption because someone looked at DNS. Another analysis discipline: separate the signal from the noise in the first 30 minutes by assigning one person to timeline duty while others contain — the timeline builder records every confirmed fact with a timestamp, which prevents the team from acting on rumors. Common tuning mistake: after a noisy false positive, teams silence the rule instead of tuning it. Silencing deletes future true positives; tuning (adding the legitimate admin tool's path as an exception, narrowing the condition) keeps the detection alive. Every alert disposition — true positive, benign true positive, false positive — should feed back into detection engineering, which is preparation for the next incident.

