<text x="386.0" y="124" font-family="ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,SF Mono,Menlo,Consolas,monospace" font-size="12" fill="#f0f6fc" text-anchor="start" font-weight="400" opacity="1.0" class="a-node" style="animation-delay:2.80s">• Run a blameless post-incident review…</text>
<text x="386.0" y="150" font-family="ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,SF Mono,Menlo,Consolas,monospace" font-size="12" fill="#f0f6fc" text-anchor="start" font-weight="400" opacity="1.0" class="a-node" style="animation-delay:3.15s">• Design and facilitate a tabletop…</text></svg><figcaption style="margin-top:.6em;color:#8b949e;font-size:.85em;text-align:center"><strong>Fig. IL29-M41-D4 — </strong>Certification exams test two things: what you can explain (left) and what you can do (right). This module&#x27;s objectives split that way — drill the left column for recall, and practice the right column hands-on under time pressure.</figcaption></figure>

A tabletop exercise is a discussion-based walkthrough of a scenario — no systems are touched, but the team talks through exactly what they would do, which exposes gaps in the playbook before a real incident does. Core IR roles to assign: the incident commander (owns decisions and the clock — not the keyboard; their job is prioritization and authorization), the technical lead (owns the hands-on investigation and containment actions), the communications lead (owns internal updates, executive briefings, and any external notifications — one voice, no freelancing), and the scribe (logs every decision, action, and timestamp — this log becomes the backbone of the after-action report). Optional: legal advisor, HR representative (for insider scenarios), and an executive sponsor who can authorize disruptive containment. Designing the exercise: write a scenario narrative (the 2 AM ransomware), define objectives ('test our containment decision process and our out-of-band comms'), then script injects — new facts delivered at intervals that force decisions. Ground rules: this is a safe space, there are no wrong answers, and 'we would call the MSP' is a valid answer only if someone actually has the number. Worked example: three injects for the ransomware tabletop. Inject 1 (T+0): 'It is 2:00 AM. Your phone rings — the monitoring system reports ransomware encrypting files on three workstations. What do you do in the first 15 minutes?' (Tests: call tree, initial containment, who declares an incident.) Inject 2 (T+30 min): 'The file server is now showing encrypted files, and the EDR console is unreachable — the admin credentials you have do not work.' (Tests: out-of-band comms, credential compromise assumptions, escalation authority.) Inject 3 (T+2 hours): 'A local news station calls asking about a rumored cyberattack at the clinic.' (Tests: communications lead discipline, executive notification, no ad-lib statements.) The facilitator keeps time, the scribe captures everything, and the debrief at the end converts observations into playbook updates. Operator gotcha: the most valuable tabletop finding is almost always embarrassingly basic — 'nobody had the MSP's after-hours number' — which is exactly why you run it before the real thing. Facilitation tips that separate a useful tabletop from a pleasant chat: keep the scenario plausible but uncomfortable — the exercise should find the gap, not confirm the plan. Time-box each inject (15-20 minutes of discussion) and cut off rambling with 'noted — what is the decision?' Inject information asymmetrically the way real incidents do: the technical lead learns the EDR is down before the incident commander does, forcing a communication test. Include at least one inject that invalidates the plan ('the phone tree's first three numbers go to voicemail') because plans always fail at the seams. For remote teams, run the tabletop over the actual out-of-band channel you would use in an incident — testing the bridge line is part of the exercise. Evaluation is observation, not grading: the facilitator and scribe watch for decision latency (how long before someone declares an incident?), authority clarity (did anyone actually make the call, or did the group drift?), communication discipline (did updates flow through the comms lead?), and evidence thinking (did anyone say 'preserve memory before wiping'?). Convert every observation into a playbook edit within a week, and re-run the same scenario in six months to verify the fixes stuck. One exercise per quarter, rotating scenarios (ransomware, business email compromise, insider data theft, third-party breach notification), builds the muscle memory that makes the real 2 AM call feel familiar instead of chaotic.

