Digital evidence decays at different rates, and the order of volatility tells you what to grab first: the most fragile evidence before the most durable. From most to least volatile: CPU registers and cache (gone in microseconds — in practice you never capture these directly); RAM contents — running processes, network connections, encryption keys, injected code (gone the moment power is lost); network state — active connections, ARP cache, routing tables (changes in seconds to minutes); running process and system state (what is executing right now); then disk — files, the MFT, event logs (persist across reboots but can be overwritten); then remote logs and monitoring data; then physical configuration; then archival backups and offline media. The practical rule: on a live suspect machine, capture memory and network state before you touch the disk, and never reboot or shut down until you have decided what volatile evidence you are willing to lose. Worked example: you walk up to a workstation the EDR flagged for credential dumping. It is powered on and logged in. Correct order: (1) photograph the screen; (2) capture RAM to external media with a memory acquisition tool; (3) record active network connections and the routing/ARP tables; (4) list running processes with full command lines; (5) only then consider disk imaging or pulling event logs. Wrong order: rebooting 'to be safe' — you just destroyed the injected code, the attacker's network sessions, and possibly the encryption keys, which were the whole case. Operator gotchas: do not run your collection tools from the suspect's own disk — run them from external read-only media so you do not overwrite unallocated space; every command you run changes the system slightly, so document each one with timestamps; and if the machine is already off, do NOT power it on to 'take a look' — image the disk first, then analyze the image. Put numbers on the decay to make the ordering instinctive. RAM: total loss at power-off, partial corruption within minutes of heavy system activity as memory gets reused. Network state (ARP cache, active connections): entries age out in minutes; DHCP leases and NAT tables churn constantly. Running processes: the list is current only while the system runs — reboot and you lose which processes were active, their PIDs, and parent-child relationships. Disk: file timestamps update on access and modification, logs roll over on busy systems within days, and unallocated space gets overwritten unpredictably. Remote logs: the most durable, but only if forwarding was configured before the incident. The live-versus-dead decision matrix: system is ON and you suspect fileless activity, credential theft, or active intrusion — treat as live, capture volatile evidence first. System is OFF and was shut down cleanly — treat as dead-box: do not boot it, image the disk write-blocked. System is ON but the incident is clearly over (old malware, user already reimaged once) — targeted artifact collection from the live system is acceptable, documented. And the documentation rule that underpins all of it: every collection action gets a timestamped log entry as you perform it, not reconstructed afterward. 'I think I imaged it around 2' is not a custody record; '14:22 — began FTK Imager acquisition of \.\PhysicalDrive0 to EVIDENCE-01' is.

