On NTFS volumes, the Master File Table (MFT) is the filesystem's index: one record per file and directory, holding the filename, timestamps, size, and where the data lives. Each MFT record carries two timestamp sets: $STANDARD_INFORMATION (SI — what Windows Explorer shows, and what timestomping malware modifies) and $FILE_NAME (FN — set at file creation and far harder to fake). That duality is a gift to investigators: if SI says a system file was 'created yesterday' but FN says 2019, someone tampered with timestamps. The MFT also remembers deleted files — deletion usually just marks the record as free without wiping it, so file names and timestamps of deleted files often survive until the record is reused. Small files may be resident (stored inside the MFT record itself); large files are non-resident (the record points to data runs elsewhere on disk). Worked example: you suspect malware dropped payload.exe into System32 last Tuesday. In the MFT you find the record: SI timestamps all show last Tuesday 03:14, but FN creation shows 2021 — classic timestomping, and itself evidence of malicious intent. The record is marked deleted, yet the filename and timestamps are intact, and the data runs point to clusters you can carve. From this one record you learn: the file existed, when it really arrived (FN birth, corroborated by other artifacts), that someone tried to hide its age, and where its content may still sit on disk. Common mistakes: trusting Explorer timestamps alone (they show SI, the forgeable set); assuming 'deleted' means 'gone' (it means 'marked free'); and analyzing the live disk instead of an image — every write you make risks overwriting the very unallocated clusters holding deleted evidence. Two companion artifacts make MFT analysis far stronger: $LogFile and $UsnJrnl. The NTFS log file ($LogFile) records transactional metadata changes — file creations, deletions, renames — and can reveal activity whose MFT record was later reused. The Update Sequence Number Journal ($UsnJrnl) logs file changes with timestamps and reasons (creation, deletion, rename, data overwrite), giving you a second witness when MFT timestamps look tampered. Together, MFT + $UsnJrnl let you catch timestomping red-handed: the journal says the file was created Tuesday while SI claims 2019. On resident versus non-resident data: small files and alternate data streams may live inside the MFT record itself (resident), which means they survive even when the file's clusters are long overwritten — malware configuration blobs hidden in ADS are recoverable this way. Large files are non-resident: the MFT stores data runs (cluster addresses), and carving follows those runs across the disk. Worked micro-example: an MFT record for invoice.pdf shows SI timestamps of last month but the $UsnJrnl entry for the same file reference number shows creation yesterday with reason 'file create' — the file is new, the timestamps are forged, and the journal entry is your proof. Common tooling note: parse the MFT with a proper parser (many free ones exist) rather than eyeballing hex — the attribute layouts are intricate, and misreading an attribute offset produces confident nonsense. Always work from the image, and always cross-check a surprising MFT finding against at least one other artifact before building conclusions on it.

