Three more Windows artifact families answer 'what ran, when, and by whom.' Prefetch: Windows tracks application startup to speed it up, storing a .pf file per executed program in C:\Windows\Prefetch. Each prefetch file records the executable name, a hash of its path, the last run time, and a run count. If malware.exe ran, prefetch usually proves it — including how many times. Windows Event Logs: the Security log records logons (event 4624 successful, 4625 failed), process creation (4688, when auditing is enabled), and account changes; the System log records service installs and shutdowns; PowerShell operational logs can capture script blocks. Registry hives: HKLM\SYSTEM\CurrentControlSet\Enum\USBSTOR proves which USB devices were connected (by serial number); Run keys (HKLM/HKCU ...\Run) show persistence; Shimcache/AppCompatCache and Amcache record program execution history going back months. Worked example: did someone plug in a personal USB drive and copy files? USBSTOR shows a SanDisk device serial 4C530001 connected Tuesday 14:02; the Security log shows logon 4624 for the user at 13:58; prefetch shows notepad.exe is irrelevant but a portable file manager ran 14 times with last run 14:20; Amcache corroborates the file manager's first execution at 14:05. Four independent artifacts, one story. Operator gotchas: event logs roll over — on a busy domain controller the Security log may only cover days, so check log size and retention settings during preparation, not during the incident; 4688 process-creation auditing is off by default on many builds, so enable it fleet-wide now via GPO; and always note the system's timezone before interpreting timestamps — a three-hour offset has sunk real investigations. Two more high-value sources complete the Windows artifact picture. Sysmon, when installed, logs process creation with full command lines and hashes, network connections per process, and driver loads — it is the single richest endpoint telemetry source on Windows, and deploying it fleet-wide via GPO is one of the highest-value preparation tasks you can do. Without Sysmon, enable 4688 process-creation auditing with command-line logging as the fallback. Amcache.hve deserves special attention: it records executed programs with their first-execution timestamps, file paths, and SHA-1 hashes, often going back months further than prefetch — when prefetch has rolled over, Amcache frequently still has the answer. For USB investigations, pair USBSTOR with the setupapi.dev.log (device install timestamps) and the Security log to build connection windows. And the preparation lesson that dwarfs all artifact knowledge: centralize your logs before the incident. Windows Event Forwarding (WEF) or a SIEM collecting Security, System, and PowerShell logs means the evidence survives even if the attacker wipes the local logs — which, per ATT&CK T1070.001, is one of the first things intruders do. Size the retention for your event volume: a domain controller generating 50,000 events a day with a 20 MB log keeps roughly hours of history. That sizing math belongs in preparation, decided once, not discovered during triage when the logs you need rolled over yesterday.

