<text x="386.0" y="150" font-family="ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,SF Mono,Menlo,Consolas,monospace" font-size="12" fill="#f0f6fc" text-anchor="start" font-weight="400" opacity="1.0" class="a-node" style="animation-delay:3.15s">• Execute a pre-reimage preservation…</text></svg><figcaption style="margin-top:.6em;color:#8b949e;font-size:.85em;text-align:center"><strong>Fig. IL29-M42-D4 — </strong>Certification exams test two things: what you can explain (left) and what you can do (right). This module&#x27;s objectives split that way — drill the left column for recall, and practice the right column hands-on under time pressure.</figcaption></figure>

The moment that defines a junior operator is the compromised workstation that 'just needs to be reimaged.' Reimaging is legitimate eradication — but reimaging before preservation destroys the evidence that explains how the attacker got in, which means the next machine falls the same way. The pre-reimage preservation runbook, in order: (1) Isolate the host from the network (EDR isolation, unplug, or disable Wi-Fi) — leave it powered on. (2) Photograph the screen and note the time, logged-on user, and visible applications. (3) Capture RAM to external media if the machine is on and the situation warrants it (credential theft, fileless malware suspected). (4) Pull the high-value targeted artifacts: Security/System event logs (export as .evtx), prefetch directory listing, Amcache/Shimcache, USBSTOR and Run keys from the registry, the MFT (or better, a full disk image if time and storage allow). (5) Record active network connections and running processes with command lines. (6) Write it all down: what you collected, when, hashes of the images, where they are stored — the start of your chain of custody. (7) Only now: reimage from known-good media (not from the machine's own recovery partition, which may be compromised), rejoin to the domain, restore user data from a clean backup, and reset the user's credentials. Worked example: help desk ticket — 'laptop slow, popups, user clicked a weird link.' The tech isolates, spends 20 minutes pulling RAM, event logs, prefetch, and registry hives to the evidence drive, documents hashes, then reimages from USB media. Analysis later shows the initial access was a fake software update — which leads to blocking that domain fleet-wide and a targeted awareness note. The 20 minutes of preservation turned one cleanup into fleet-wide prevention. Operator gotchas: 'the user needs it back today' pressure is real — the runbook exists so preservation is fast and routine, not a debate; never reimage from the local recovery partition on a compromised host; and tell the user what you are doing and why — 'I am saving diagnostic data before rebuilding' beats a mysterious 20-minute delay. The communication around reimaging matters as much as the technical steps. Tell the user what is happening in plain words: 'Your machine shows signs of compromise, so I am saving diagnostic data before rebuilding it clean — this takes about 20 minutes, then you will have a fresh system.' That single sentence prevents the 'IT took my laptop and will not say why' resentment cycle. Give a realistic return time and keep it; if analysis will take days, say so and provide a loaner. After reimaging, validate before returning: confirm the build came from known-good media (verify the image hash if your process supports it), confirm EDR and patching are active, restore only user data (documents, not executables or installers) from a backup predating the incident, and reset the user's password with MFA re-enrollment. Then close the loop back into detection: the preserved artifacts should produce at least one improvement — a blocked domain, a new detection rule, a GPO change, or an awareness note. The reimage checklist is therefore not the end of the incident but a handoff: preservation → rebuild → return → improve. Track the metric that proves the runbook's value: mean time from 'suspected compromise' to 'preserved and rebuilding.' When leadership asks what the security program delivers, 'we preserve evidence on every suspected host within 30 minutes and every case improves a control' is an answer backed by process, not adjectives. And keep the runbook itself under version control with a review date — the checklist that nobody updates quietly becomes the checklist nobody follows.

