Threat intelligence speaks in two kinds of indicators. An indicator of compromise (IOC) is a forensic artifact: a file hash, an IP address, a domain name, a filename, a registry key. IOCs are precise and easy to share — you can block a hash in seconds — but they are brittle: the attacker changes the hash with every build, rotates IPs hourly, and registers fresh domains daily. An indicator of attack (IOA), also called a behavioral indicator, describes what the attacker DOES: 'a Microsoft Office application spawning a command shell,' 'credential dumping from LSASS memory,' 'a scheduled task created for persistence.' IOAs are harder to write but far more durable — the attacker's tools change weekly, their tactics change yearly. Mature defenses layer both: IOCs for fast blocking of known-bad artifacts, IOAs for catching the behavior even when every artifact is new. Worked example: a ransomware crew hits three companies. Company A shares the payload hash (IOC) — useful for exactly one variant. Company B shares the behavior chain (IOA): phishing document → macro → PowerShell download cradle → credential theft → lateral movement via SMB. Company C, which never saw the hash, still detects the attack because its defenses key on the PowerShell-from-Office behavior. The hash was dead in a day; the behavior is still catching that crew's attacks months later. Common mistakes: building a detection program on IOC feeds alone (you are always one variant behind); treating every IOC as permanent (stale IOCs cause false positives — expire them); and the reverse error — dismissing IOCs entirely (they are cheap, fast, and excellent for triage even if they are not a strategy). Security researcher David Bianco's Pyramid of Pain organizes indicators by how much pain changing them causes the attacker — and it is the best mental model for why IOAs beat IOCs. At the bottom (trivial to change): hashes, IPs, domains. In the middle: network and host artifacts, tools. At the top (painful to change): tactics, techniques, and procedures — the adversary's actual tradecraft. Your detection program should climb the pyramid deliberately: block the easy IOCs automatically, but invest analyst time at the TTP level, because that is where detections survive the attacker's next build. Sharing intelligence follows the same logic. IOCs are shared as simple lists and feeds; richer context travels in structured formats like STIX/TAXII, and frameworks like ATT&CK give both sides a common language so 'we saw T1059.001 from a Word macro' means the same thing to every team. A practical sharing example: after your ransomware case, you publish an internal bulletin with the phishing sender domain (IOC, expires in 30 days), the malicious hash (IOC), and the behavior chain — Office macro → encoded PowerShell → LSASS access → scheduled task (IOAs, kept as detections permanently). The IOCs age out; the behavior detections keep paying. One caution on consuming external intel: every feed contains false positives and stale entries. Before blocking a feed IOC fleet-wide, check age, source confidence, and whether the artifact could be shared infrastructure (a compromised legitimate site is not the same as an attacker domain). Intelligence is evidence to evaluate, not orders to execute.

