<text x="636.0" y="104" font-family="ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,SF Mono,Menlo,Consolas,monospace" font-size="11" fill="#8b949e" text-anchor="middle" font-weight="400" opacity="1.0" class="a-node" style="animation-delay:3.85s">Note 4</text></svg><figcaption style="margin-top:.6em;color:#8b949e;font-size:.85em;text-align:center"><strong>Fig. IL29-M43-D1 — </strong>Read the notes in order — IOCs vs IOAs: artifacts versus… lays the foundation and Mapping a blocked action to… brings it together. Each note assumes the vocabulary of the ones before it.</figcaption></figure>

MITRE ATT&CK is a public knowledge base of how real adversaries behave, organized as matrices (Enterprise covers Windows, macOS, Linux, and cloud). Its two core concepts: tactics are the adversary's objectives — the 'why' at each stage (get in, run code, stay in, steal credentials, move around, take data, cause damage). Techniques are the specific methods — the 'how' (spearphishing attachment, PowerShell, scheduled tasks, credential dumping). Each technique has an ID like T1059 (Command and Scripting Interpreter) and sub-techniques like T1059.001 (PowerShell) — the numbering is just an address, not a ranking. Procedures are the specific implementations seen in the wild. The mental model: tactic = chapter heading, technique = section, procedure = the worked example. You do not need to memorize the matrix; you need to be fluent in the ten or so tactics and techniques you will actually encounter on an endpoint fleet, and know how to look up the rest. Worked example: an alert says 'winword.exe launched powershell.exe with an encoded command.' Tactic: Execution (the adversary is trying to run code). Technique: T1059 Command and Scripting Interpreter, sub-technique T1059.001 PowerShell. The procedure — encoded PowerShell from a Word macro — is also a Defense Evasion signal (obfuscation). One observed action, two tactic mappings, one precise technique ID. That is ATT&CK fluency: behavior in, structured language out. Operator gotchas: mapping is analyst judgment, not math — two analysts can map the same event to adjacent techniques, and that is fine as long as you cite the observed behavior; technique IDs change across ATT&CK versions, so always note which version you used; and never present a mapping as certainty to stakeholders — 'consistent with' beats 'this was definitely.' ATT&CK comes in matrices for different domains: Enterprise (Windows, macOS, Linux, cloud, containers — the one you will live in), Mobile, and ICS. Within Enterprise, platforms let you filter techniques to your reality — a Windows fleet cares about a different subset than a cloud-native shop. Navigating the knowledge base is a skill: start from the tactic pages to learn the story, drill into techniques for detection and mitigation guidance (each technique page lists data sources, detection ideas, and mitigations — free consulting from the people who catalogued the adversary), and check the procedure examples to see how real groups implement it. Note the version: ATT&CK updates regularly, techniques get renamed, split, or deprecated, so cite the version in your documentation the way you would cite any reference. A second core concept worth naming: ATT&CK also catalogues mitigations and data sources per technique, which makes it a defensive planning tool, not just an attacker encyclopedia. When your coverage map shows a blind spot on T1053.005 Scheduled Task, the technique page itself tells you which logs to collect and which mitigations apply. Common beginner trap: treating ATT&CK as a compliance checklist ('we must cover all techniques') — it is a knowledge base for prioritization, not a standard to certify against. The goal is risk-informed coverage of the techniques most likely used against your environment, defended in depth, with the reasoning documented. That reasoning — why these techniques, why these controls — is what a panel respects, not the percentage covered.

