<text x="360.0" y="178" font-family="ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,SF Mono,Menlo,Consolas,monospace" font-size="11" fill="#8b949e" text-anchor="middle" font-weight="400" opacity="1.0" class="a-node" style="animation-delay:2.45s">plain</text></svg><figcaption style="margin-top:.6em;color:#8b949e;font-size:.85em;text-align:center"><strong>Fig. IL29-M43-D2 — </strong>The vocabulary this module installs — IOCs, IOAs, artifacts…. If you can define each term in one plain sentence, the module&#x27;s objectives are within reach.</figcaption></figure>

ATT&CK tactics roughly follow the order of a real intrusion. Learn them as a story: Reconnaissance (learning about the target) and Resource Development (buying domains, building malware) happen before contact. Initial Access is the break-in — most commonly spearphishing attachment or link, but also valid accounts and exposed services. Execution runs the attacker's code (malicious macros, PowerShell). Persistence makes it survive reboots (scheduled tasks, Run keys, new services). Privilege Escalation gets higher rights. Defense Evasion hides the activity (obfuscation, disabling logging, timestomping). Credential Access steals logins (dumping LSASS, keylogging). Discovery maps the environment (who is admin, what shares exist). Lateral Movement spreads to other machines (SMB, RDP, stolen credentials). Collection gathers the target data. Command and Control maintains the remote channel. Exfiltration sends data out. Impact is the finale — encryption, destruction, or defacement. Worked example — the full walk for a clinic ransomware case: Initial Access: phishing email with 'invoice.doc' (T1566.001 Spearphishing Attachment). Execution: macro launches PowerShell (T1059.001). Persistence: scheduled task 'OfficeUpdate' (T1053.005). Defense Evasion: PowerShell obfuscation, event log clearing (T1070.001). Credential Access: LSASS dump (T1003.001). Discovery: network share enumeration. Lateral Movement: SMB with stolen admin credentials (T1021.004). Impact: data encrypted for ransom (T1486). Notice how one intrusion touches eight tactics — which is why a single well-placed control (blocking Office from spawning shells) disrupts the chain at Execution before the other seven happen. Common mistakes: memorizing IDs without understanding the story (the panel will ask 'walk me through an attack,' not 'recite T-numbers'); treating the tactic order as mandatory (attackers skip, reorder, and loop — cloud attacks may start at Valid Accounts); and mapping only the finale (Impact) while ignoring the six earlier tactics where detection was possible. A second walkthrough cements the pattern — this time a cloud-flavored intrusion, to show the tactics travel. Initial Access: valid credentials bought from an infostealer log (T1078 Valid Accounts) — no phishing needed. Execution: the attacker runs commands through the cloud provider's own management console. Persistence: a new federated identity provider trust (cloud equivalent of a scheduled task). Defense Evasion: deleting cloud audit logs. Credential Access: dumping the identity store. Discovery: enumerating storage buckets and roles. Lateral Movement: assuming roles across accounts. Collection and Exfiltration: copying snapshots to an external account. Impact: deleting the originals. Same tactic story, different techniques — which is exactly why tactic-level thinking transfers across environments while technique specifics change. From the defender's chair, each tactic suggests a control family: Initial Access → phishing-resistant MFA and conditional access; Execution → application control; Persistence → change monitoring on startup mechanisms; Credential Access → credential guarding and tiered admin; Lateral Movement → segmentation; Exfiltration → egress monitoring and data classification. Work that mapping for your own environment once, and you have a security architecture justified in adversary language rather than vendor language. The interview version of this walkthrough takes 90 seconds: pick the endpoint ransomware chain, name each tactic as you go, and land on the punchline — 'and that is why blocking at Execution, before persistence and credential access, is the highest-leverage control we have.' Brevity with structure signals mastery; rambling through fourteen tactics signals memorization.

