<text x="386.0" y="98" font-family="ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,SF Mono,Menlo,Consolas,monospace" font-size="12" fill="#f0f6fc" text-anchor="start" font-weight="400" opacity="1.0" class="a-node" style="animation-delay:2.80s">• Use ATT&amp;CK coverage mapping to find…</text></svg><figcaption style="margin-top:.6em;color:#8b949e;font-size:.85em;text-align:center"><strong>Fig. IL29-M43-D4 — </strong>Certification exams test two things: what you can explain (left) and what you can do (right). This module&#x27;s objectives split that way — drill the left column for recall, and practice the right column hands-on under time pressure.</figcaption></figure>

The panel does not need you to recite the matrix — they need to see operator fluency: behavior in, structured reasoning out, stakeholder translation on demand. Expect prompts like 'a user is angry we blocked their macro — walk us through it' or 'how would you explain ringfencing to a clinic manager.' The winning structure: state the observed behavior precisely, map it to tactic and technique with the reasoning out loud ('Excel spawning an encoded shell is Execution via T1059.001, with obfuscation suggesting Defense Evasion'), explain why the control fired in adversary terms ('this is the exact step ransomware uses between the phishing email and encryption — blocking here kills the chain before lateral movement'), and close with the stakeholder translation ('so to the user I would say...'). A second high-value move: explain why default-deny controls are ATT&CK-efficient — one policy ('Office apps cannot spawn script interpreters') simultaneously frustrates Execution, Persistence setup, and parts of Defense Evasion across dozens of techniques, whereas IOC blocking plays whack-a-mole with one hash at a time. That single insight demonstrates you think in behaviors, not artifacts. Worked example — panel prompt: 'Why did we block PowerShell for this user?' Strong answer: 'The block fired because winword.exe launched powershell.exe with an encoded command — that maps to Execution, technique T1059.001, and the encoding suggests Defense Evasion. In plain terms: the document tried to run a hidden program, which is step two of nearly every ransomware chain after the phishing email. We stopped it before it could establish persistence or touch credentials. I checked the email source, confirmed it was not a legitimate workflow, and explained to the user what to watch for.' Calm, structured, no jargon overload, ends with the human. Common mistakes: name-dropping technique IDs without explaining the behavior (fluency is reasoning, not vocabulary); freezing when you cannot recall an ID (say 'I would look up the exact sub-technique — the tactic is Execution' — honesty beats bluffing); and badmouthing the user ('they clicked a phish' — the user is the customer). Tie ATT&CK fluency to STAR stories from your own experience, because the panel hires on evidence, not vocabulary. 'Tell me about a time you handled a security alert' becomes: Situation — 'EDR flagged encoded PowerShell from Word on a workstation.' Task — 'Determine if it was malicious and scope it.' Action — 'I mapped it to Execution T1059.001 with Defense Evasion indicators, checked the delivering email, found it reached twelve inboxes, and had IT purge it while I verified no other host executed it.' Result — 'One host reimaged, zero spread, and I wrote the detection rule that now alerts on any Office-spawned interpreter fleet-wide.' Eighty seconds, behavior to mapping to outcome to improvement — that is the whole module in one answer. Prepare three such stories (a block you investigated, a mapping that found a blind spot, a stakeholder explanation that worked) and you can answer most technical-behavioral questions by reaching for one. Also prepare two questions for THEM that show operator thinking: 'How does the team currently map detections to ATT&CK — is there a coverage map I would contribute to?' and 'What does the escalation path look like when a block might be a false positive affecting clinical operations?' Questions like these signal you think about their real problems, not just your interview performance. Final trap to avoid: never inflate. If your ATT&CK experience is this course plus lab work, say so proudly — 'I have mapped fifty scenarios in training and built coverage maps for a fictional clinic; I am fluent in the workflow and honest about the boundary of my experience.' Panels can smell bluffing; they reward the candidate who knows exactly what they know.

