<text x="360.0" y="218" font-family="ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,SF Mono,Menlo,Consolas,monospace" font-size="11" fill="#8b949e" text-anchor="middle" font-weight="400" opacity="1.0" class="a-node" style="animation-delay:7.35s">visual guides</text>
<text x="576" y="218" font-family="ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,SF Mono,Menlo,Consolas,monospace" font-size="11" fill="#3fb950" text-anchor="end" font-weight="600" opacity="1.0" class="a-node" style="animation-delay:7.70s">2</text></svg><figcaption style="margin-top:.6em;color:#8b949e;font-size:.85em;text-align:center"><strong>Fig. IL29-M43-D3 — </strong>What&#x27;s inside “Threat Intelligence &amp; MITRE ATT&amp;CK Mapping”: 7 teaching notes and 5 objectives, backed by 27 practice components. Notes carry the teaching; everything else converts reading into recall.</figcaption></figure>

Phishing is a crafted lie delivered at scale, and every phish has the same anatomy: a spoofed or lookalike sender, a subject engineered for emotion, a pretext that explains why you are being contacted, a call to action (click, reply, open), and a payload (credential-harvesting page, malicious attachment, or simply a conversation that leads to a wire transfer). Learn to dissect each layer. Sender: display-name spoofing ('Acme Billing <billing@acme-secure-pay.com>') exploits the fact that most people read the display name, not the address; lookalike domains swap characters (acme-biIIing.com with capital i's, or acrnе.com with a Cyrillic e). Headers (in the full message source) reveal the true return path and authentication results. Body: urgency ('your account will be suspended in 24 hours'), authority (a fake CEO, a vendor, IT), and specificity stolen from public sources (your real invoice number from a breached vendor). Links: hover to reveal the true destination — credential phish hides behind 'View Invoice' buttons pointing at unrelated domains. Attachments: Office documents with macros, HTML attachments that render a fake login page locally. Worked example — dissecting a fictional phish line by line: Subject 'ACTION REQUIRED: Past-due invoice #INV-88412.' From: 'Acme Corp Billing <billing@acme-billlng.com>' — note the double-l, lookalike domain. Body thanks you for your business and warns service suspension Friday — urgency plus a real-sounding invoice number. The 'Pay Now' button links to acme-billing-portal.ru — country mismatch, unrelated domain. Attachment 'invoice_88412.html' — an HTML attachment is itself a red flag; opened locally it shows a pixel-perfect Microsoft login asking for credentials. Verdict in one minute: lookalike sender domain, urgency, mismatched link, HTML attachment — four independent red flags. Common mistakes: judging by logos and polish (attackers copy branding perfectly); trusting the display name; and forwarding the phish to a colleague to ask 'is this real?' — which spreads it. Report it through the reporting channel instead. Three phishing variants deserve names because they change the defense. Spearphishing targets a specific person with researched detail — the invoice from your actual vendor, the email from your actual manager (possibly from their compromised account). Whaling targets executives with authority-flavored lures — the fake board memo, the 'urgent acquisition' wire request. Business email compromise (BEC) often has no payload at all — just a conversation: 'Are you available? I need a favor,' followed by gift-card or wire instructions. BEC defeats every attachment scanner ever built because there is nothing malicious to scan — the defense is purely procedural (verify out-of-band, require dual authorization for payments). A newer variant: QR-code phishing ('quishing') — the malicious link hides inside a QR code the email scanner cannot easily read, and the victim scans it with a phone that may lack the company's email protections. Defenses adapt: QR codes in email get the same suspicion as links, and phones get the reporting habit too. Attachment discipline in plain terms: treat unexpected attachments as guilty until verified — Office macros, HTML attachments, ISO/ZIP containers, and especially multi-stage 'open the HTML, click the link, enter credentials' chains. Worked micro-example of BEC defended: the CFO's 'urgent wire' email arrives Friday at 4:55 PM. Procedure: no wire moves on email instruction alone — the finance staffer calls the CFO on the known number. Ninety seconds, attack dead. That procedure, written down and followed under pressure, is worth more than any email filter against BEC.

