Vishing (voice phishing) and smishing (SMS phishing) move the con to channels where people have fewer defenses — no hover-to-inspect on a phone call, and text messages carry an intimacy that email lost a decade ago. The classic vishing script is the fake IT helpdesk: 'Hi, this is Mike from IT, we are seeing suspicious logins on your account, I need to verify your identity — can you read me the code that just arrived on your phone?' Every element is engineered: authority (IT), urgency (suspicious logins), and a request that sounds like verification but is actually the attacker completing a login as you with the MFA code you just read aloud. MFA fatigue attacks (push bombing) spam your phone with approval prompts at 2 AM until you tap approve just to make it stop. Smishing lures impersonate package delivery ('your parcel is held — confirm address'), banks ('unusual transaction — reply YES'), and toll agencies — always with a link to a lookalike site or a callback number the attacker controls. Caller ID is spoofable, so 'the number matched the bank' proves nothing. Worked example — annotated vishing transcript: Caller: 'This is Daniel from IT security, employee ID 4421.' (Red flag 1: unsolicited authority claim — real IT does not cold-call for credentials; employee IDs are theater.) 'We detected your account logging in from Russia.' (Red flag 2: fear + urgency.) 'I am going to send a verification code — read it back so I know it is really you.' (Red flag 3: the inversion — a legitimate verifier never asks you to prove yourself by surrendering a secret; the code is YOUR authentication, and reading it hands over your session.) Correct response, rehearsed: 'I will call the help desk back on the internal number and open a ticket.' Then hang up. Defense in depth: publish the rule 'IT will never ask for your password or MFA code, ever' — a simple, absolute, testable policy — and give people the internal callback number on a card. Common mistakes: debating the caller (engagement is the trap — hang up); calling back the number they gave you (it rings the attacker); and assuming older or non-technical staff are the only targets (executives get whaling calls; IT staff get 'vendor support' calls). Two emerging twists raise the stakes on voice and SMS fraud. AI voice cloning lets an attacker reproduce a trusted voice — a manager, a family member — from seconds of public audio, turning 'verify the voice' from a defense into a vulnerability. The countermeasure is a shared secret or callback procedure agreed in advance: 'any unusual request gets verified by calling back on the known number, no matter how real the voice sounds.' Families use a code word; companies use the callback rule. SIM swapping is the heavier variant: the attacker convinces the carrier to move your number to their SIM, intercepting calls and SMS codes — which is why SMS-based MFA is the weakest form and app-based or hardware MFA is the upgrade path worth pushing. On smishing, the current lure catalog: package delivery ('customs fee due'), toll and DMV notices, bank fraud alerts, and 'wrong number' conversation starters that build rapport over days before the ask (pig-butchering style). The defense pattern never changes: the message supplies urgency and contact details; you supply independent verification. Organization-level defenses: publish the absolute rules ('IT never asks for codes,' 'the bank never texts links'), put the real contact numbers where people can find them in the moment (intranet banner, wallet card, lock-screen wallpaper for shared devices), and run a vishing drill the way you run phishing simulations — a scripted fake IT call to a sample of staff, with kind debriefing. People who have heard the script once recognize it forever; people who have only read about it often do not.

