<text x="360.0" y="178" font-family="ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,SF Mono,Menlo,Consolas,monospace" font-size="11" fill="#8b949e" text-anchor="middle" font-weight="400" opacity="1.0" class="a-node" style="animation-delay:2.45s">Vishing</text></svg><figcaption style="margin-top:.6em;color:#8b949e;font-size:.85em;text-align:center"><strong>Fig. IL29-M44-D2 — </strong>The vocabulary this module installs — Phishing, anatomy, dissecting…. If you can define each term in one plain sentence, the module&#x27;s objectives are within reach.</figcaption></figure>

Not every con arrives as a message. Pretexting is a fabricated scenario used to extract information or access: the 'auditor' who needs the Wi-Fi password for their inspection, the 'vendor technician' here to service the copier who needs a network drop, the caller doing a 'customer satisfaction survey' who walks the receptionist into confirming employee names, titles, and email formats — each answer a building block for the next attack. Baiting dangles something the victim wants: USB drives labeled 'Q3 bonuses' left in the parking lot (when plugged in, they run malware or simply phone home to prove the point), or a 'free software crack' download that is pure trojan. Tailgating (piggybacking) is physical: following an employee through a badge-controlled door with arms full of boxes and a friendly smile — no exploit kit required. The defense is procedural, not technical: a verification habit ('I verify unexpected requests through a known channel'), a challenge culture for physical access ('I do not hold the door — everyone badges in, no exceptions, and that rule protects you too'), and clean-desk / clean-USB policies with the reasoning explained. Worked example — the auditor pretext, defended: A well-dressed visitor tells the front desk she is from the 'state inspection office' and needs the guest Wi-Fi password plus a look at the server room. Trained response: 'I would be glad to help — let me verify your visit with our office manager, who schedules all inspections.' The verification goes through the known internal channel, not the phone number on her business card. If legitimate, the five-minute check costs nothing; if not, it just stopped a physical intrusion. Common mistakes: treating politeness as a security control ('he seemed nice'); verifying through attacker-supplied contact details (the card, the callback number, the email in the signature — all attacker-controlled); and assuming physical attacks are rare — for targeted intrusions they are a standard opening move. Elicitation — the conversational side of pretexting — deserves its own study because it targets helpfulness, not gullibility. The elicitor never asks for the secret directly; they ask around it: 'I am new here, who handles the VPN issues?' → 'Oh, Marcus does, marcus.j@...' → each answer builds the org chart for the next attack. Techniques include flattery ('you clearly know how things really work here'), feigned ignorance ('I am so lost with this new system'), and the false statement ('so the backups run on Fridays, right?' — corrected to the true schedule by a helpful employee). Defenses: the 'need-to-know pause' (before answering, ask 'why do they need this and who are they?'), and deflecting to public channels ('that is on our website' or 'let me connect you with our office manager'). Physical security drills make this concrete: hire or role-play a friendly visitor attempting tailgating and information elicitation, then debrief — the receptionist who challenged the visitor becomes the example, praised publicly. Visitor policy in plain terms, posted at the desk: every visitor signs in, wears a badge, is escorted beyond the lobby, and the Wi-Fi password is never given verbally to unverified visitors. And the clean-USB rule with its reasoning explained: 'Unknown USB devices go to IT unopened — not because we distrust you, but because attackers count on curiosity, and IT has safe ways to check.' Rules with reasons get followed; rules without reasons get resented and bypassed.

