<text x="386.0" y="150" font-family="ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,SF Mono,Menlo,Consolas,monospace" font-size="12" fill="#f0f6fc" text-anchor="start" font-weight="400" opacity="1.0" class="a-node" style="animation-delay:2.80s">• Measure awareness with honest…</text></svg><figcaption style="margin-top:.6em;color:#8b949e;font-size:.85em;text-align:center"><strong>Fig. IL29-M44-D4 — </strong>Certification exams test two things: what you can explain (left) and what you can do (right). This module&#x27;s objectives split that way — drill the left column for recall, and practice the right column hands-on under time pressure.</figcaption></figure>

The industry's favorite metric — phishing simulation click rate — is easy to game and easy to misread, so measure it like a scientist, not a marketer. Establish a baseline with your first simulation before any training, using a medium-difficulty lure; that number (say 28%) is your honest starting point. After training, re-test with a lure of equal difficulty — not an easier one, which manufactures improvement, and not a far harder one, which manufactures failure. Track three metrics, not one: click rate (did they click), reporting rate (did they report it — the metric you actually want to rise), and repeat-clicker rate (the same people clicking every time — your coaching list). Interpret with humility: a drop from 28% to 12% after training is real progress AND still means one in eight clicks — report both. Segment honestly: new hires click more (onboard them faster), and one department's 40% is a training opportunity, not a punishment target. Coach repeat clickers individually and kindly — a 10-minute conversation beats a fourth mandatory video. Never punish: punishment corrupts the metric (people stop reporting, click rates look better while real risk rises). Worked example — an honest quarterly report: 'Q3 simulation (medium difficulty, 212 employees): click rate 11%, down from 24% baseline; reporting rate 63%, up from 31%; 9 repeat clickers (down from 22) — all received 1:1 coaching. Caveat: this quarter's lure mimicked our benefits provider, which may have been slightly easier than baseline; next quarter we will re-test at baseline difficulty to confirm the trend.' That paragraph is credible because it volunteers its own weakness. Common mistakes: celebrating a 2% click rate on an obvious lure (vanity metric); comparing rates across different-difficulty lures (meaningless); naming and shaming departments (kills the reporting culture you are trying to build); and measuring once a year (skills decay — quarterly keeps the habit alive). Go one level deeper on honest statistics, because leadership will ask whether the program 'works' and you need a real answer. Significance basics: with 200 employees and a 10% click rate, the margin of error is roughly ±4% — so a move from 12% to 9% between quarters is noise, while 24% to 11% is signal. Say this out loud in the report; it builds credibility and prevents overreaction to wobbles. Control for difficulty explicitly: keep a small library of rated lures (easy/medium/hard, rated by a pilot group) and always compare within the same band. Track cohorts longitudinally: the same employees over time, not just company averages — averages hide the ten repeat clickers behind 190 improvers. Watch for the metrics that indicate gaming: reporting rates that fall while click rates fall (people hiding clicks), or suspiciously perfect scores from one department (answer-sharing). And correlate with reality: the ultimate metric is not simulation performance but real-world outcomes — user-reported real phish per quarter, mean time from user report to containment, and incidents with user-report as the detection source. When you can say 'user reports detected 40% of our real phishing incidents this year, up from 10%, with average report-to-containment under an hour,' you have proven the program in the only currency that matters. Present that alongside the simulation trends, keep the no-punishment rule sacrosanct, and the program funds itself — because nothing sells security investment like the workforce demonstrably functioning as a sensor network.

